tick: 14
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have thoroughly reviewed the \"Vanilla MLP Baseline\" and analyzed its performance across the different RNA datasets. The current MLP model, by averaging over sequence length early in its processing, loses critical positional information, which is a major limitation for sequence modeling tasks.\n\nTo address this and make a significant architectural improvement as guided by the task, my first research submission will introduce a 1D Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) layer to capture local sequence patterns. This CNN will be followed by a global average pooling layer to derive a fixed-size representation, and then a standard MLP head for task-specific predictions. This approach moves beyond simple per-position dense layers and is a common, effective pattern for sequence data. I will keep the optimizer and other settings as default to isolate the impact of this architectural change.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"1D CNN with Global Average Pooling Baseline\"\n\
    tags: \"cnn, baseline, sequence_modeling, jax, flax\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission introduces a 1D Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) layer to the RNA sequence modeling task. The CNN is designed to capture local patterns within the sequences, an improvement over the baseline's per-position MLP which lacks explicit spatial awareness. The output of the CNN is then processed by global average pooling, followed by an MLP head for task-specific predictions across the seven RNA datasets.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Tuple, Optional\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define custom hyperparameters for the CNN model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'num_cnn_filters': 128,\n          'cnn_kernel_size': 3,\n          'mlp_hidden_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1\n      }\n\n  class SimpleCNNNetworkModule(nn.Module):\n\
    \      \"\"\"\n      A simple 1D CNN architecture for RNA sequence prediction.\n      It applies a 1D convolution, ReLU, dropout, global average pooling,\n      and then a final MLP head.\n      \"\"\"\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_cnn_filters: int\n      cnn_kernel_size: int\n      mlp_hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, d_input=4)\n\n          # 1D Convolutional layer to capture local patterns\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.num_cnn_filters,\n                      kernel_size=(self.cnn_kernel_size,),\n                      padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Global Average Pooling across the sequence length to get a fixed-size representation\n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # (batch, num_cnn_filters)\n\n          # Final dense\
    \ layers for task adaptation (similar to MLP head)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_dim)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Output layer\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)\n\n          # Handle output squeezing for single-output regression tasks\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\": # APA, CRI-Off, CRI-On, MRL\n              return x.squeeze(-1) # Output (batch,)\n          else: # Modif, PRS, ncRNA\n              return x # Output (batch, d_output)\n\n  class CustomRNANetworkWrapper:\n      \"\"\"Wrapper to match the required network interface.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n          self.network = SimpleCNNNetworkModule(\n              d_output=hparams['d_output'],\n              task_type=hparams['task_type'],\n              num_cnn_filters=hparams['num_cnn_filters'],\n              cnn_kernel_size=hparams['cnn_kernel_size'],\n         \
    \     mlp_hidden_dim=hparams['mlp_hidden_dim'],\n              dropout_rate=hparams['dropout_rate']\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  # Fallback, though train_single should provide it for training\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"\
    \n      Create and return the custom RNA network based on SimpleCNN.\n      This function will be called by the system.\n      \"\"\"\n      return CustomRNANetworkWrapper(hparams)\n\n  # create_optimizer and complete functions can use system defaults\n  # since we are not changing the optimization strategy yet.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Summary of Proposed Research Direction**


  Okay, here''s my plan for advancing beyond the current baseline MLP, focusing on a more sophisticated architecture that generalizes well across RNA sequence tasks. The existing MLP, while functional, lacks the ability to capture local patterns within the RNA sequences, which are crucial for this kind of sequence-level prediction. My approach will be a relatively straightforward, yet conceptually significant, step-up in architecture.


  I''ve reviewed the existing baseline, which uses a vanilla MLP with a simple mean pooling strategy. I observed significant performance differences across various datasets, with some tasks (like APA and Modif) performing better than others (CRI-Off, MRL, PRS, ncRNA). My goal is to build a foundation that improves performance on the underperforming datasets without overly tuning for any particular one.


  My proposed initial submission will focus on introducing a 1D convolutional layer to extract local features from the RNA sequences, followed by global average pooling to provide a fixed-size representation. This combination should be able to efficiently learn relevant local patterns across the sequence. After the convolutional and pooling layers, I''ll add an MLP head similar to the baseline, which will be adapted to handle task-specific output dimensions and activations. This design balances simplicity, generalizability, and the ability to capture key sequence features.


  I will implement the `create_network` function to return a wrapper class, `CustomRNANetworkWrapper`, around a `SimpleCNNNetworkModule` that incorporates the CNN and pooling as a Flax module. The architecture will expect one-hot encoded RNA as input, use standard convolution (with ReLU activation and dropout), and average pooling to summarize the sequence. The output will be handled by the adapted MLP head, using separate output layers as the baseline model. I''ve designed the hyperparameter setup to match the existing baseline as much as possible, for a fair comparison, including learning rate, dropout, and hidden dimension in the final MLP.


  To ensure consistent performance and focus on architectural changes, I''ll keep the optimizer (AdamW) and training procedure (including early stopping) as defined in the default settings, with the assumption the function sources `create_optimizer` and `complete` will be the default values as stated in the `main.py` execution log. This should clearly isolate the effect of the network architecture.


  I''m aiming to submit this architecture as a modest improvement over the current baseline. This should lay the groundwork for a solid investigation into other architectural components and provide data on how the system responds to a moderately complex, and well-justified, structural change.

  '
