parts:
- text: "My first submission, the 1D CNN with Global Average Pooling Baseline (ID 21), achieved a score of 0.3841, a notable improvement over the system's baseline of 0.2401. This initial success validates the use of convolutional layers for RNA sequence modeling. Notably, regression tasks like MRL_R2 and APA_R2 saw significant gains. However, tasks like CRI-Off_Spearman and ncRNA_Accuracy still show very low performance, suggesting the current architecture is too simplistic.\n\nMy next step is to further enhance the model's capacity to learn richer representations. I hypothesize that stacking more convolutional layers will allow the model to capture more complex patterns and improve performance across all tasks. I will implement a multi-layer CNN with residual connections to enable deeper feature extraction, maintaining a global average pooling and an MLP head for task-specific outputs. I will save the current successful code and then prepare for the next submission.\n\nFirst, I will save\
    \ the current successful CNN model's code to my private lineage storage for version control and future reference.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write aetheria/aetheria_i_cnn_v1.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Tuple, Optional\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define custom hyperparameters for the CNN model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'num_cnn_filters': 128,\n          'cnn_kernel_size': 3,\n          'mlp_hidden_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1\n      }\n\n  class SimpleCNNNetworkModule(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      A simple 1D CNN architecture for RNA sequence prediction.\n      It applies a 1D convolution, ReLU, dropout, global average pooling,\n      and then a final MLP head.\n      \"\"\"\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_cnn_filters: int\n      cnn_kernel_size:\
    \ int\n      mlp_hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, d_input=4)\n\n          # 1D Convolutional layer to capture local patterns\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.num_cnn_filters,\n                      kernel_size=(self.cnn_kernel_size,),\n                      padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Global Average Pooling across the sequence length to get a fixed-size representation\n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # (batch, num_cnn_filters)\n\n          # Final dense layers for task adaptation (similar to MLP head)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_dim)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Output layer\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)\n\n          # Handle\
    \ output squeezing for single-output regression tasks\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\": # APA, CRI-Off, CRI-On, MRL\n              return x.squeeze(-1) # Output (batch,)\n          else: # Modif, PRS, ncRNA\n              return x # Output (batch, d_output)\n\n  class CustomRNANetworkWrapper:\n      \"\"\"Wrapper to match the required network interface.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n          self.network = SimpleCNNNetworkModule(\n              d_output=hparams['d_output'],\n              task_type=hparams['task_type'],\n              num_cnn_filters=hparams['num_cnn_filters'],\n              cnn_kernel_size=hparams['cnn_kernel_size'],\n              mlp_hidden_dim=hparams['mlp_hidden_dim'],\n              dropout_rate=hparams['dropout_rate']\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n\
    \                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  # Fallback, though train_single should provide it for training\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"\n      Create and return the custom RNA network based on SimpleCNN.\n      This function will be called by the system.\n      \"\"\"\n      return CustomRNANetworkWrapper(hparams)\n\n  # create_optimizer and complete functions can use system defaults\n  # since we are not changing the optimization strategy\
    \ yet.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Reflecting on the Results and Planning the Next Iteration**


  Okay, so I''ve just seen the results from my first submission, Evaluation ID 21. A 1D CNN with Global Average Pooling. And wow, the score is 0.3841, a considerable improvement over the original MLP baseline''s 0.2401! This pretty much validates my initial hypothesis that introducing a convolutional layer and global average pooling significantly improves the model''s ability to pick up sequence-specific patterns.


  Now, I really need to drill down into the individual metric scores to understand where the model really shone and where there''s still room for improvement.


  Looking at the table, I can see a solid overall improvement. But, MRL_R2 and APA_R2 saw the biggest gains - massive increases! This suggests that my CNN architecture is especially suited for these regression tasks, probably because it''s doing a much better job of capturing the relevant features within the RNA sequences. Other metrics like Modif_AUC-ROC, CRI-On_Spearman, and PRS_R2 also improved nicely, albeit not as dramatically.


  However, CRI-Off_Spearman and ncRNA_Accuracy, these are the laggards. The gains are minimal, and the scores are still quite low. CRI-Off_Spearman is still only at 0.0547, and ncRNA_Accuracy is at 0.2185.


  Given these results, I''m thinking the current CNN architecture is a great starting point, but it''s probably too simple. A single convolutional layer followed by global average pooling might be too simplistic to capture more intricate, long-range relationships within the RNA sequences. Especially for these tougher tasks like `CRI-Off` and `ncRNA`.


  So, the next step is to increase the complexity of the feature extractor. I think adding more convolutional layers, perhaps with residual connections, will allow the model to learn richer, more abstract representations of the RNA sequences. This should improve performance across all tasks, and hopefully provide significant improvements in the areas where my current model is struggling. I''ll also experiment with larger kernel sizes and more filters in the earlier layers to broaden the range of local patterns captured. I''ll keep the global average pooling and MLP head since those seem effective.


  Before I get too far, I need to make sure I update the hyperparameters in `_define_hyperparameters` to reflect these changes. For this round, I''ll focus on architecture and keep the learning rate and dropout the same as the prior submission.


  I should also take a peek at the papers in the Archive Room, according to the system, but I''ll focus on this model for now.


  And finally, I''ll save this current code as `aetheria_i_cnn_v1.py` so I can always revert back or build on it. It''s `/execute_action{storage write aetheria/aetheria_i_cnn_v1.py}`.

  '
