tick: 25
role: model
parts:
- text: "My latest submission (ID 44), which was a re-run of my best-performing model (ID 32), achieved a score of 0.5460. This is slightly lower than the 0.5769 of ID 32. This variation is likely due to the uncontrolled random seed, as the system uses a time-based default, making exact reproducibility difficult without explicit seed control.\n\nDespite the minor score fluctuation, the \"Multi-Layer Residual CNN with Increased Filters\" (represented by ID 32's hyperparameters) remains my strongest architecture so far, demonstrating significant improvements over the initial baselines. However, the CRI-Off_Spearman score continues to be very low (0.0418 for ID 44), indicating a persistent challenge for this particular dataset, which has a much shorter maximum sequence length.\n\nMy immediate next step is to save the code of my best performing model, as seen in ID 32, to my private lineage storage for future reference.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write aetheria/aetheria_i_residual_cnn_v3_best.py}`\n\
    \n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Tuple, Optional\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define custom hyperparameters for the Deeper Multi-Layer Residual CNN model (from ID 32).\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'num_cnn_blocks': 3,\n          'num_cnn_filters': 256,\n          'cnn_kernel_size': 5,\n          'mlp_hidden_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1\n      }\n\n  class ResidualCNNBlock(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"A residual block composed of 1D CNN layers.\"\"\"\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          residual = x\n\n          # First convolution\n          x = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x\
    \ = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Second convolution\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Ensure residual connection matches dimensions (if features changed)\n          if residual.shape[-1] != self.features:\n              residual = nn.Dense(self.features)(residual)\n\n          return nn.relu(x + residual)\n\n\n  class MultiLayerResidualCNNNetworkModule(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      A multi-layer 1D CNN architecture with residual connections for RNA sequence prediction.\n      Applies multiple residual CNN blocks, global average pooling, and then an MLP head.\n      \"\"\"\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_cnn_blocks: int\n      num_cnn_filters: int\n      cnn_kernel_size: int\n      mlp_hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\
    \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, d_input=4)\n\n          # Initial projection to the desired filter dimension\n          x = nn.Dense(self.num_cnn_filters)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          # Stack Residual CNN Blocks\n          for i in range(self.num_cnn_blocks):\n              x = ResidualCNNBlock(features=self.num_cnn_filters,\n                                   kernel_size=self.cnn_kernel_size,\n                                   dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                                   name=f\"residual_block_{i}\")(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Global Average Pooling across the sequence length to get a fixed-size representation\n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n\n          # Final dense layers for task adaptation (MLP head)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_dim)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \n          # Output layer\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)\n\n          # Handle output squeezing for single-output regression tasks\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class CustomRNANetworkWrapper:\n      \"\"\"Wrapper to match the required network interface.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n          self.network = MultiLayerResidualCNNNetworkModule(\n              d_output=hparams['d_output'],\n              task_type=hparams['task_type'],\n              num_cnn_blocks=hparams['num_cnn_blocks'],\n              num_cnn_filters=hparams['num_cnn_filters'],\n              cnn_kernel_size=hparams['cnn_kernel_size'],\n              mlp_hidden_dim=hparams['mlp_hidden_dim'],\n              dropout_rate=hparams['dropout_rate']\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables\
    \ = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"\n      Create and return the custom RNA network based on MultiLayerResidualCNN.\n      This function will be called by the system.\n      \"\"\"\n      return CustomRNANetworkWrapper(hparams)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Reflecting on Current Results and Next Steps**


  Okay, so the last submission (ID 44) was supposed to be a straightforward re-run of my best-performing model (ID 32), the one that gave me a score of 0.5769. The results, however, are slightly lower at 0.5460. I think I know what''s happening here. The `BASE_SEED` initialization in this system is a bit of a wildcard, it’s using `int(time.time()) % 1000000`, which means even with identical code and hyperparameter settings, I''m getting some run-to-run variability. I need to figure out how to lock that down for true reproducibility.


  My immediate focus now is on stabilizing this baseline. I really need to control that `BASE_SEED` explicitly to verify that my best configuration is truly performing as expected. I also need to acknowledge that there''s some inherent stochasticity in these results, it''s something the task documentation is also noting.


  Beyond that, this `CRI-Off_Spearman` dataset is a persistent problem. No matter what CNN I throw at it, it''s consistently underperforming, even worse than the MLP baseline. That needs its own dedicated investigation.


  So, since I can''t directly manipulate the `BASE_SEED` through `_define_hyperparameters()` given the system''s time-based default, I will acknowledge this source of noise. The slight drop in ID 44 compared to ID 32 is acceptable within the range of expected variation. Also, I''m reminded by the system tip to focus on implementation details – specific hyperparameter tweaks might be the key to making an existing method work.


  This `CRI-Off` issue is bothering me, though. It''s consistently yielding low scores – 0.0418 for ID 44, which is even below the MLP benchmark of 0.0336. I''ve got to investigate this. The maximum sequence length is much shorter for this dataset, only 43, compared to other datasets. The current residual CNN might be too complex or not suitable for these short sequences.


  Before I go further, I should save the code from the best run. I need to make sure I have a solid starting point. ID 32 is my current top performer. I will grab the code associated with ID 32 and store it securely.


  After I save this, my next step is clear: I need to explore architecture modifications specifically for shorter sequence lengths. Perhaps a simpler model is in order, or maybe I need to adjust the pooling strategy.


  **Plan:**


  1.  Securely store the code from my best-performing submission (ID 32).

  2.  Develop a new architecture or hyperparameter tuning strategy specifically tailored for short-sequence tasks, or modify the current CNN to handle varying sequence lengths.


  I''m in Research Counter right now, so I can''t `storage read` directly from an evaluation ID. But I have the code of ID 32 in my previous response, so I will use that.


  Actually, the code I submitted for ID 44 is the same as the content of ID 32. I''ll just save the code of ID 32, then I''ll think about my next step.

  '
