- text: 'My latest submission (ID 70), a hybrid CNN-Transformer model, yielded a score of 0.5600, which is still below my best (ID 32: 0.5769). The Transformer component, as implemented, did not provide the expected overall gains and even worsened performance on some tasks, notably CRI-Off, indicating that this complex addition needs more careful integration or tuning, or might not be suitable universally.


    Given the consistent performance of my Multi-Layer Residual CNN from ID 32/55, and the advice to focus on implementation details and hyperparameter tuning, I will revert to that strong baseline. I will re-submit that exact architecture but with a slightly reduced learning rate (`0.0005` instead of `0.001`). This is a common strategy to allow models to converge more carefully and potentially find better local optima, especially when the current architecture is already robust. This is a low-risk experiment to try before making further architectural changes, and it doesn''t violate the "no dataset-specific methods" rule.


    I will read my saved best model `aetheria_i_residual_cnn_v3_best.py` from storage and then use that content in my submission, modifying only the `learning_rate`.


    `/execute_action{storage read aetheria/aetheria_i_residual_cnn_v3_best.py}`'
thinking_content: '**Re-evaluating and Refining My Approach**


  Okay, so I''ve just reviewed the results from the latest submission (ID 70), the CNN-Transformer hybrid. While the idea seems promising in theory, it clearly underperformed my previous best (ID 32), with a score of 0.5600 compared to 0.5769. It''s especially concerning that CRI-Off_Spearman is still struggling. I had a hunch that a full Transformer block on these short sequences (max length 43) might be overkill, and the data supports that. Adding this complex layer seemed to drag down overall performance.


  My initial thought was to find a more targeted way to incorporate global context without the full computational cost of self-attention.  The "Academic Integrity" section, though, is presenting me with a challenge: my solution needs to generalize across datasets, so I can''t just create dataset-specific solutions.


  Looking back, the failure of the previous attempt with a simpler self-attention layer (ID 50) also gave me pause. It makes me hesitant to jump into another form of attention without a better understanding of why it didn''t work initially. It''s worth remembering that the prompt mentioned that "tweaking hyperparameters and other specifics may be enough to make an existing method work effectively."


  So, I''m going to take a step back and revisit my most successful model (ID 32). This Deeper Multi-Layer Residual CNN achieved 0.5769, and at least got CRI-Off to 0.0346, which is better than some other results. The key here is to simplify things and build on a solid foundation.


  My next submission will be built off this:


  1.  **Baseline Re-establishment:** I''ll load the exact configuration from my best-performing model (saved as `aetheria_i_residual_cnn_v3_best.py`) to give me a reliable starting point. This ensures consistency and lets me clearly assess the impact of any changes.

  2.  **Targeted Hyperparameter Optimization:** Instead of introducing new architectural complexities, I''m going to focus on optimizing the existing hyperparameters within this proven architecture. I want to try a slightly lower learning rate (e.g., 0.0005, instead of the current 0.001) to see if that allows for more stable and finer convergence, especially given the smaller datasets and potentially complex loss landscape. Dropout doesn''t need to be touched. CNN kernel size of 5 seems to be the best.


  This is a conservative approach, but it''s grounded in the data. The goal is to get the best out of my current architecture and avoid chasing potentially detrimental complexities. I will load the saved model file and only change the learning rate.

  '
