parts:
- text: "My latest submission (ID 75), with a reduced learning rate, yielded a lower overall score (0.5500) than my best (ID 32: 0.5769), indicating that this particular hyperparameter change was not beneficial. The results across all my experiments suggest that the Multi-Layer Residual CNN architecture (from ID 32/55) provides the most stable and highest performance so far. The CRI-Off task continues to be a bottleneck, showing consistently low scores.\n\nTo further improve the model, particularly for tasks sensitive to sequence order and to provide more context for shorter sequences, I will now implement **fixed sinusoidal positional embeddings**. This will encode the position of each nucleotide within the sequence, supplementing the one-hot encoded input. This is a common and effective technique in sequence modeling that can benefit convolutional networks. I will apply these embeddings before the first dense layer of the CNN. I will revert to the original `learning_rate` of `0.001` as\
    \ used in my best runs.\n\nFirst, I will save the code from the current submission (ID 75) for a complete record of my experiments.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write aetheria/aetheria_i_residual_cnn_lr_0005.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Tuple, Optional\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define custom hyperparameters for the Deeper Multi-Layer Residual CNN model with a reduced learning rate.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.0005, # Reduced from 0.001\n          'num_cnn_blocks': 3,\n          'num_cnn_filters': 256,\n          'cnn_kernel_size': 5,\n          'mlp_hidden_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1\n      }\n\n  class ResidualCNNBlock(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"A residual block composed of 1D CNN layers.\"\"\"\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n\
    \      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          residual = x\n\n          # First convolution\n          x = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Second convolution\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Ensure residual connection matches dimensions (if features changed)\n          if residual.shape[-1] != self.features:\n              residual = nn.Dense(self.features)(residual)\n\n          return nn.relu(x + residual)\n\n\n  class MultiLayerResidualCNNNetworkModule(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      A multi-layer 1D CNN architecture with residual connections for RNA sequence prediction.\n      Applies multiple residual\
    \ CNN blocks, global average pooling, and then an MLP head.\n      \"\"\"\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_cnn_blocks: int\n      num_cnn_filters: int\n      cnn_kernel_size: int\n      mlp_hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, d_input=4)\n\n          # Initial projection to the desired filter dimension\n          x = nn.Dense(self.num_cnn_filters)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          # Stack Residual CNN Blocks\n          for i in range(self.num_cnn_blocks):\n              x = ResidualCNNBlock(features=self.num_cnn_filters,\n                                   kernel_size=self.cnn_kernel_size,\n                                   dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                                   name=f\"residual_block_{i}\")(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Global Average Pooling across the sequence length to get a fixed-size\
    \ representation\n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n\n          # Final dense layers for task adaptation (MLP head)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_dim)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Output layer\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)\n\n          # Handle output squeezing for single-output regression tasks\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class CustomRNANetworkWrapper:\n      \"\"\"Wrapper to match the required network interface.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n          self.network = MultiLayerResidualCNNNetworkModule(\n              d_output=hparams['d_output'],\n              task_type=hparams['task_type'],\n              num_cnn_blocks=hparams['num_cnn_blocks'],\n              num_cnn_filters=hparams['num_cnn_filters'],\n              cnn_kernel_size=hparams['cnn_kernel_size'],\n\
    \              mlp_hidden_dim=hparams['mlp_hidden_dim'],\n              dropout_rate=hparams['dropout_rate']\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"\n      Create and return the custom RNA network based on MultiLayerResidualCNN.\n\
    \      This function will be called by the system.\n      \"\"\"\n      return CustomRNANetworkWrapper(hparams)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analysis and Next Steps for Sequence Modeling**


  Okay, so the evaluation results for submission 75 are in, and they''re disappointing. A score of 0.5500 is significantly below my best (ID 32 at 0.5769) and even slightly under the re-established baseline (ID 55: 0.5754). It looks like simply dropping the learning rate to 0.0005 wasn''t the magic bullet I was hoping for; it might have actually *hindered* convergence on some of the tasks.


  Right now, I''m fairly certain that the Multi-Layer Residual CNN architecture, as configured in IDs 32 and 55, is the most robust starting point I have. The more complex additions like self-attention (ID 50) and hybrid pooling (ID 62) didn''t deliver the overall gains I was aiming for, even though they showed promise in some isolated areas. Similarly, the hyperparameter tweaks like kernel size or increased dropout (ID 40) didn''t help and in some cases, hurt performance.


  The persistent low performance on the CRI-Off task is really bugging me. This short-sequence dataset seems to be a real sticking point. The current strategy of general architectural modifications appears to have hit a wall, and now it feels like I''m making it worse. This leads me to believe I need a more dataset-specific approach *without* sacrificing generalizability, if possible.


  So, here''s the plan. I''m going back to the strongest performing configuration (ID 32, as verified by ID 55) and saving the current code for completeness, even if the results are mediocre. This will give me a point of comparison.


  Next, I''m focusing on improving how the input sequence is represented. Sequence data often benefits from positional embeddings. Since my CNNs operate locally, and explicit attention didn''t give me consistent results, encoding position information directly could be the key to helping the network learn more effectively.  This could be particularly beneficial for tasks where sequence order or specific positions are critical, and potentially give a little more context to the shorter sequences as well.


  I''m going to add fixed sinusoidal positional encoding to the one-hot encoded RNA input before it hits the initial `nn.Dense` layer. It''s a standard practice in Transformers, but I''m thinking it could provide valuable relational information for my CNNs too. I''ll also revert the learning rate back to 0.001 (from IDs 32 and 55).

  '
