thinking_content: '**The Waiting Game: Simplifying for CRI-Off**


  Alright, here''s the situation: I''m staring at the clock, waiting for the results of submission ID 86. This is a crucial moment. ID 80, the one with those sinusoidal positional embeddings, gave us our best score so far – a respectable 0.6218. But, as we all know, it didn''t really crack the CRI-Off issue. That''s the thorn in my side right now.


  So, with ID 86, I''ve tried a different tack. I''ve stripped down the CNN backbone – going with just 2 blocks, 128 filters, and a 3x3 kernel. The idea is that the CRI-Off problem, with its short sequences, might be getting bogged down in too much complexity. Maybe this simplification, combined with the positional embeddings, will be the magic bullet. Fingers crossed!


  Once the results are in, I''ll dive in: First, I''ll compare the overall score to ID 80 and the other benchmark, ID 32. But the *real* test is CRI-Off''s Spearman score. Did this simplification actually help there? Then, of course, I''ll check all the other metrics. I don''t want to see a big drop in performance across the board just for a win on CRI-Off.


  Based on the numbers, I''ll decide where to go next. If ID 86 significantly improves CRI-Off without tanking the overall score, we might be onto something. Then, I can start fine-tuning this simpler architecture. But if CRI-Off is still a mess or the overall score takes a huge hit, it means this simplification isn''t the key. I''ll need to go back to the drawing board – maybe explore different architectural approaches or tackle this CRI-Off thing from a totally fresh perspective.


  The "Automated System Tips" haven''t been too helpful. Just suggesting tweaks, and I''ve already done learning rate adjustments. Now, it is time for architectural capacity tweaks.


  So, it''s just a waiting game now. And I hate waiting.

  '
