parts:
- text: "My latest submission (ID 88), combining positional embeddings with hybrid global pooling, yielded an overall score of 0.6183, which is lower than my previous best (ID 86: 0.6224). Critically, the `CRI-Off_Spearman` score plummeted to 0.0049, confirming that the hybrid pooling strategy, in this configuration, was detrimental for this dataset.\n\nTherefore, my immediate next step is to re-establish the optimal baseline set by submission ID 86 (\"Simpler Residual CNN with Positional Embeddings and Reduced Capacity\"). This approach achieved the highest overall score and significantly improved `CRI-Off_Spearman`. Re-submitting this known good configuration is crucial to ensure a stable reference point for future, more targeted research.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Simpler Residual CNN with Positional Embeddings and Reduced Capacity (Re-run of ID 86 Baseline)\"\ntags: \"cnn, residual, positional-encoding, capacity-reduction, baseline, sequence-modeling, jax, flax\"\
    \nabstract: |\n  This submission is a direct re-run of the best-performing model (ID 86) to re-establish the optimal baseline. The architecture features a simpler Multi-Layer Residual CNN (2 blocks, 128 filters, kernel 3) augmented with sinusoidal positional embeddings. This configuration previously achieved the highest overall score and significantly improved performance on short-sequence tasks like CRI-Off. Re-running ensures a stable reference point for future targeted research after experiments with alternative pooling yielded lower performance.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Tuple, Optional\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define custom hyperparameters for the Simpler Residual CNN with Positional Embeddings.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'num_cnn_blocks': 2,\n          'num_cnn_filters': 128,\n         \
    \ 'cnn_kernel_size': 3,\n          'mlp_hidden_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'positional_embedding_dim': 128\n      }\n\n  class PositionalEncoding(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, seq_len: int):\n          position = jnp.arange(seq_len, dtype=jnp.float32)[:, jnp.newaxis]\n          div_term = jnp.exp(jnp.arange(0, self.d_model, 2, dtype=jnp.float32) * -(jnp.log(10000.0) / self.d_model))\n          \n          pe = jnp.zeros((seq_len, self.d_model), dtype=jnp.float32)\n          pe = pe.at[:, 0::2].set(jnp.sin(position * div_term))\n          pe = pe.at[:, 1::2].set(jnp.cos(position * div_term))\n\n          return pe\n\n  class ResidualCNNBlock(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"A residual block composed of 1D CNN layers.\"\"\"\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          residual = x\n\n          # First convolution\n\
    \          x = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Second convolution\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Ensure residual connection matches dimensions (if features changed)\n          if residual.shape[-1] != self.features:\n              residual = nn.Dense(self.features)(residual)\n\n          return nn.relu(x + residual)\n\n\n  class MultiLayerResidualCNNWithPositionalEncodingNetworkModule(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      A multi-layer 1D CNN architecture with residual connections,\n      fixed sinusoidal positional encoding, global average pooling, and an MLP head.\n      \"\"\"\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n\
    \      num_cnn_blocks: int\n      num_cnn_filters: int\n      cnn_kernel_size: int\n      mlp_hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      positional_embedding_dim: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, d_input=4)\n\n          if self.positional_embedding_dim != self.num_cnn_filters:\n              raise ValueError(f\"positional_embedding_dim ({self.positional_embedding_dim}) must match num_cnn_filters ({self.num_cnn_filters}) for direct addition.\")\n          \n          # Initial projection to the desired filter dimension\n          x = nn.Dense(self.num_cnn_filters)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          # Add Positional Encoding\n          current_seq_len = x.shape[1]\n          pe = PositionalEncoding(d_model=self.num_cnn_filters)(current_seq_len)\n          x = x + pe[jnp.newaxis, :, :]\n\n          # Stack Residual CNN Blocks\n          for i in range(self.num_cnn_blocks):\n             \
    \ x = ResidualCNNBlock(features=self.num_cnn_filters,\n                                   kernel_size=self.cnn_kernel_size,\n                                   dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                                   name=f\"residual_block_{i}\")(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Global Average Pooling across the sequence length to get a fixed-size representation\n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n\n          # Final dense layers for task adaptation (MLP head)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_dim)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Output layer\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class CustomRNANetworkWrapper:\n      \"\"\"Wrapper to match the required network interface.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n\
    \          self.network = MultiLayerResidualCNNWithPositionalEncodingNetworkModule(\n              d_output=hparams['d_output'],\n              task_type=hparams['task_type'],\n              num_cnn_blocks=hparams['num_cnn_blocks'],\n              num_cnn_filters=hparams['num_cnn_filters'],\n              cnn_kernel_size=hparams['cnn_kernel_size'],\n              mlp_hidden_dim=hparams['mlp_hidden_dim'],\n              dropout_rate=hparams['dropout_rate'],\n              positional_embedding_dim=hparams['positional_embedding_dim'],\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params':\
    \ params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"\n      Create and return the custom RNA network based on MultiLayerResidualCNNWithPositionalEncoding.\n      This function will be called by the system.\n      \"\"\"\n      return CustomRNANetworkWrapper(hparams)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Resubmission Strategy and Code Review**


  Okay, so I''ve got the code for the best-performing model, ID 86, saved and ready to go as `aetheria_i_residual_cnn_positional_v2_stable_best.py`. My current plan, which I realized needed some reevaluation, was to combine the best features from different models: the simpler Residual CNN with sinusoidal positional embeddings from ID 86, with hybrid global pooling from an earlier experiment, and with the hyperparameters like learning rate, dropout, and MLP hidden dimension as planned in ID 88.


  I had prepared the combined code based on the architecture of ID 88. But now I''ve done a more detailed review of my past results. I''m focusing on the significant drop in `CRI-Off_Spearman` for ID 88, which used hybrid pooling. While hybrid pooling *did* show promise for `ncRNA_Accuracy` in ID 62, it seems to have had a detrimental effect, especially for CRI-Off.


  Given this, and after rereading my past prompts, I''ve realized that I''d actually *already* made the decision to re-submit the code from ID 86. I had a momentary lapse and forgot that I decided in the previous turn to re-establish the baseline with the code from ID 86, after the poor performance of ID 88.


  So, the action here is clear: I need to submit the code from `aetheria_i_residual_cnn_positional_v2_stable_best.py`, which is ID 86. This submission is a re-run of that experiment. It''s important to re-establish the strongest baseline I have, given the inconsistent results with more complex models like ID 88. This step aligns with the system message to not give up on methods too easily, and it will give me a known point of comparison to refine future submissions.


  After this resubmission, I''ll then review the potential reasons why CRI-Off performs poorly with some experiments. I am going to investigate why hybrid pooling, or a deeper architecture, is detrimental in these instances. It could be that max pooling or other elements are introducing too much noise for those short sequences.


  I''ve already got the code from the previous turns. So, I will proceed to read the content of the file and submit its content for evaluation, explicitly stating it''s a re-run of ID 86.

  '
