parts:
- text: "My latest submission (ID 114) successfully replicated Noema I's SOTA model, achieving a score of 0.6741. This is a strong baseline, but I observed that `CRI-Off_Spearman` remains consistently low (0.0371), indicating a persistent challenge for this dataset.\n\nNoema I's paper specifically suggested exploring \"cross-half interaction pooling\" to address `CRI-Off`. An ablation (Eval 91) by Noema I with \"Mean + Low-Rank Bilinear Pooling (r=16, no MHA)\" achieved 0.6775.\n\nTherefore, my next research step is to implement this \"Cross-Half Bilinear Pooling\" strategy. I will replace the current `concat(mean, MHA)` pooling with a concatenation of Global Average Pooling and a Low-Rank Bilinear Pooling applied across the two halves of the sequence. This is designed to specifically target interaction structures like the sgRNA-target in CRI-Off. I will use Noema I's recommended hyperparameters for the convolutional backbone (5 dilated DSConv blocks with increasing dilations `[1,2,4,8,16]`,\
    \ `kernel_size=7`, `num_cnn_filters=256`, no positional encoding). The `bilinear_rank` will be set to 16, and the `mlp_hidden_dim` to 256.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Noema-Inspired SOTA: Dilated DSConv with Cross-Half Bilinear Pooling (No-PE)\"\ntags: \"cnn, dilated, depthwise-separable, bilinear-pooling, cross-half, no-positional-encoding, jax, flax\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission implements a pooling strategy inspired by Noema I's ablation (Eval 91), specifically targeting the persistent low performance on tasks like CRI-Off. It replaces the previous hybrid pooling (Mean+MHA) with a \"Cross-Half Bilinear Pooling\" layer, which concatenates Global Average Pooling with a low-rank bilinear interaction computed between the two halves of the sequence. This aims to capture specific pairwise interaction patterns, without relying on absolute positional encoding or dataset-specific logic, while retaining the proven dilated depthwise-separable CNN backbone.\ncontent:\
    \ |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Tuple, Optional, Sequence\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define custom hyperparameters based on Noema I's SOTA (Eval 74) with Bilinear Pooling adjustments.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'num_cnn_blocks': 5,\n          'num_cnn_filters': 256,\n          'cnn_kernel_size': 7,\n          'dilation_rates': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16],\n          'bilinear_rank': 16, # New hyperparameter for low-rank bilinear pooling\n          'mlp_hidden_dim': 256, # Input to this layer will be num_cnn_filters + bilinear_rank = 256 + 16 = 272\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1\n      }\n\n  class DepthwiseSeparableConv1D(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"A Flax implementation of Depthwise Separable Convolution 1D.\"\"\"\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      strides: int = 1\n      padding: str = 'VALID'\n      dilation:\
    \ int = 1 \n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x: (batch, seq_len, in_features)\n          depthwise_conv = nn.Conv(\n              features=x.shape[-1],\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              strides=(self.strides,),\n              padding=self.padding,\n              feature_group_count=x.shape[-1],\n              name=\"depthwise_conv\",\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.lecun_normal(),\n              bias_init=nn.initializers.zeros,\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,) \n          )(x)\n          \n          pointwise_conv = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.features,\n              kernel_size=(1,),\n              strides=(1,),\n              padding='VALID',\n              name=\"pointwise_conv\",\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.lecun_normal(),\n              bias_init=nn.initializers.zeros\n          )(depthwise_conv)\n          return pointwise_conv\n\n  class ResidualDSConvBlock(nn.Module):\n\
    \      \"\"\"A residual block composed of Dilated Depthwise Separable 1D CNN layers.\"\"\"\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dilation_rate: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          residual = x\n\n          x = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          x = DepthwiseSeparableConv1D(features=self.features,\n                                       kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\n                                       dilation=self.dilation_rate,\n                                       padding='SAME',\n                                       name=\"dsconv1\")(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x = DepthwiseSeparableConv1D(features=self.features,\n                                       kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\n                                       dilation=self.dilation_rate,\n                                       padding='SAME',\n\
    \                                       name=\"dsconv2\")(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          if residual.shape[-1] != self.features:\n              residual = nn.Dense(self.features)(residual)\n\n          return nn.relu(x + residual)\n\n  class CrossHalfBilinearPooling(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Cross-half bilinear pooling layer for sequence data.\n      Splits the sequence into two halves and computes a low-rank bilinear interaction.\n      \"\"\"\n      output_rank: int # The 'r' in low-rank bilinear pooling\n      features: int # Input feature dimension (num_cnn_filters)\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, features)\n          batch_size, seq_len, features = x.shape\n\n          # Split sequence into two halves\n          mid_point = seq_len // 2\n          x_left = x[:, :mid_point, :]  # (batch, mid_point,\
    \ features)\n          x_right = x[:, mid_point:, :] # (batch, seq_len - mid_point, features)\n\n          # Average pool each half to get single vector representations\n          h_left = jnp.mean(x_left, axis=1)  # (batch, features)\n          h_right = jnp.mean(x_right, axis=1) # (batch, features)\n\n          # Linear projections for low-rank bilinear pooling\n          u_left = nn.Dense(self.output_rank, name=\"u_left\")(h_left)   # (batch, output_rank)\n          v_right = nn.Dense(self.output_rank, name=\"v_right\")(h_right) # (batch, output_rank)\n\n          # Element-wise product\n          bilinear_output = u_left * v_right # (batch, output_rank)\n\n          bilinear_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(bilinear_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          return bilinear_output\n\n  class NoemaSOTABilinearNetworkModule(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Architecture inspired by Noema I's SOTA for RNA sequence prediction,\n      using Cross-Half Bilinear Pooling\
    \ and no positional encoding.\n      \"\"\"\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_cnn_blocks: int\n      num_cnn_filters: int\n      cnn_kernel_size: int\n      dilation_rates: Sequence[int]\n      bilinear_rank: int\n      mlp_hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, d_input=4)\n\n          # Initial projection to the desired filter dimension\n          x = nn.Dense(self.num_cnn_filters)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          # Stack Residual DSConv Blocks with increasing dilations\n          for i in range(self.num_cnn_blocks):\n              x = ResidualDSConvBlock(features=self.num_cnn_filters,\n                                      kernel_size=self.cnn_kernel_size,\n                                      dilation_rate=self.dilation_rates[i],\n                                      dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                                 \
    \     name=f\"residual_dsconv_block_{i}\")(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Global Average Pooling\n          avg_pool_features = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # (batch, num_cnn_filters)\n\n          # Cross-Half Bilinear Pooling\n          bilinear_features = CrossHalfBilinearPooling(output_rank=self.bilinear_rank,\n                                                        features=self.num_cnn_filters,\n                                                        dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                                                        name=\"cross_half_bilinear_pooling\")(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          # Concatenate pooling features\n          x = jnp.concatenate([avg_pool_features, bilinear_features], axis=-1) # (batch, num_cnn_filters + bilinear_rank)\n\n          # Final dense layers for task adaptation (MLP head)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_dim)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \n          # Output layer\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class CustomRNANetworkWrapper:\n      \"\"\"Wrapper to match the required network interface.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n          self.network = NoemaSOTABilinearNetworkModule(\n              d_output=hparams['d_output'],\n              task_type=hparams['task_type'],\n              num_cnn_blocks=hparams['num_cnn_blocks'],\n              num_cnn_filters=hparams['num_cnn_filters'],\n              cnn_kernel_size=hparams['cnn_kernel_size'],\n              dilation_rates=hparams['dilation_rates'],\n              bilinear_rank=hparams['bilinear_rank'],\n              mlp_hidden_dim=hparams['mlp_hidden_dim'],\n              dropout_rate=hparams['dropout_rate'],\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key =\
    \ random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"\n      Create and return the custom RNA network based on NoemaSOTABilinearNetworkModule.\n      This function will be called by the system.\n      \"\"\"\n      return CustomRNANetworkWrapper(hparams)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Implementation Plan for Cross-Half Bilinear Pooling**


  Okay, I''ve got the Noema-inspired SOTA code saved. Now it''s time to implement that "cross-half bilinear pooling" strategy. This is the next step to push the performance.


  Here''s the plan: I''ll stick with the Dilated Depthwise Separable CNN backbone from Noema''s SOTA (5 blocks, dilations [1,2,4,8,16], kernel size 7, 256 filters) and no positional encoding. The main change is replacing the current `concat(avg_pool, mha_pool)` with the new "Cross-Half Bilinear Pooling."


  This new pooling will split the sequence in two halves, compute a low-rank bilinear outer product between these halves, and then concatenate that result with global average pooling, just like in Noema''s Eval 91 ablation. The bilinear rank (`bilinear_rank`) will be a hyperparameter; Noema used `r=16` in Eval 91, so that''s the starting point.


  Here are the specific hyperparameters: `learning_rate`: 0.001, `num_cnn_blocks`: 5, `num_cnn_filters`: 256, `cnn_kernel_size`: 7, `dilation_rates`: [1, 2, 4, 8, 16], `bilinear_rank`: 16, and `dropout_rate`: 0.1.


  The output from `concat(avg_pool, bilinear_pool)` will have a dimension of `num_cnn_filters + bilinear_rank = 256 + 16 = 272`. The `mlp_hidden_dim` for the MLP head can still be 256, the previous code handled a size of 512, because `mlp_hidden_dim` refers to the dimension of the final MLP hidden layer and not the input size.


  I''ve already defined a new `CrossHalfBilinearPooling` module, and now I just need to integrate it into the main network.  The key is to correctly split the sequence, apply linear layers, perform the outer product, and make sure dimensions align for concatenation. I''m going to assume even sequence lengths for now to keep it simple, and I can use `jnp.split` since the data will have padding to reach `max_seq_len` in all cases.


  I''m feeling pretty confident about this - the approach is based on a clear ablation study. I''m hoping this specialized pooling improves the performance, and I will be looking to get at least 0.6801 or higher with the current SOTA, which is a good target. The main potential pitfall is correct handling of the sequence length and making sure the dimensions for the linear layers work correctly for the bilinear part.

  '
