role: model
parts:
- text: "My latest submission (ID 120), incorporating \"cross-half bilinear pooling,\" yielded a score of 0.6658, which is slightly lower than my previous best (ID 114: 0.6741) and still doesn't address the low `CRI-Off_Spearman` performance effectively. This indicates that this specific pooling mechanism, or its interaction with the existing architecture, is not an improvement over the `concat(mean, MHA)` approach.\n\nNoema I's original SOTA (Eval 74) used `concat(mean, MHA)` pooling. Before I try to modify the CRI-Off strategy further, it's crucial to first re-establish my highest performing baseline. This will ensure I have a stable reference point for future targeted experiments.\n\nTherefore, my next action is to re-submit the exact code from my ID 114 submission, which uses Noema I's best configuration (`concat(mean, MHA)` pooling) to ensure I have a robust and high-performing baseline.\n\nFirst, I will save the code from the current submission (ID 120) to my private lineage storage\
    \ for a complete record of this experiment's outcome.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write aetheria/aetheria_i_noema_cross_half_bilinear_v1.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Tuple, Optional, Sequence\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define custom hyperparameters based on Noema I's SOTA (Eval 74) with Bilinear Pooling adjustments.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'num_cnn_blocks': 5,\n          'num_cnn_filters': 256,\n          'cnn_kernel_size': 7,\n          'dilation_rates': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16],\n          'bilinear_rank': 16, # New hyperparameter for low-rank bilinear pooling\n          'mlp_hidden_dim': 256, # Input to this layer will be num_cnn_filters + bilinear_rank = 256 + 16 = 272\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1\n      }\n\n  class DepthwiseSeparableConv1D(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"A Flax implementation\
    \ of Depthwise Separable Convolution 1D.\"\"\"\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      strides: int = 1\n      padding: str = 'VALID'\n      dilation: int = 1 \n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x: (batch, seq_len, in_features)\n          depthwise_conv = nn.Conv(\n              features=x.shape[-1],\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              strides=(self.strides,),\n              padding=self.padding,\n              feature_group_count=x.shape[-1],\n              name=\"depthwise_conv\",\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.lecun_normal(),\n              bias_init=nn.initializers.zeros,\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,) \n          )(x)\n          \n          pointwise_conv = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.features,\n              kernel_size=(1,),\n              strides=(1,),\n              padding='VALID',\n              name=\"pointwise_conv\",\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.lecun_normal(),\n\
    \              bias_init=nn.initializers.zeros\n          )(depthwise_conv)\n          return pointwise_conv\n\n  class ResidualDSConvBlock(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"A residual block composed of Dilated Depthwise Separable 1D CNN layers.\"\"\"\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dilation_rate: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          residual = x\n\n          x = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          x = DepthwiseSeparableConv1D(features=self.features,\n                                       kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\n                                       dilation=self.dilation_rate,\n                                       padding='SAME',\n                                       name=\"dsconv1\")(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x = DepthwiseSeparableConv1D(features=self.features,\n                               \
    \        kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\n                                       dilation=self.dilation_rate,\n                                       padding='SAME',\n                                       name=\"dsconv2\")(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          if residual.shape[-1] != self.features:\n              residual = nn.Dense(self.features)(residual)\n\n          return nn.relu(x + residual)\n\n  class CrossHalfBilinearPooling(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Cross-half bilinear pooling layer for sequence data.\n      Splits the sequence into two halves and computes a low-rank bilinear interaction.\n      \"\"\"\n      output_rank: int # The 'r' in low-rank bilinear pooling\n      features: int # Input feature dimension (num_cnn_filters)\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, features)\n          batch_size, seq_len,\
    \ features = x.shape\n\n          # Split sequence into two halves\n          mid_point = seq_len // 2\n          x_left = x[:, :mid_point, :]  # (batch, mid_point, features)\n          x_right = x[:, mid_point:, :] # (batch, seq_len - mid_point, features)\n\n          # Handle cases where one half might be empty or smaller due to odd seq_len\n          h_left = jnp.mean(x_left, axis=1) if mid_point > 0 else jnp.zeros((batch_size, features))\n          h_right = jnp.mean(x_right, axis=1) if (seq_len - mid_point) > 0 else jnp.zeros((batch_size, features))\n\n          # Linear projections for low-rank bilinear pooling\n          u_left = nn.Dense(self.output_rank, name=\"u_left\")(h_left)\n          v_right = nn.Dense(self.output_rank, name=\"v_right\")(h_right)\n\n          # Element-wise product\n          bilinear_output = u_left * v_right\n\n          bilinear_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(bilinear_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          return bilinear_output\n\
    \n  class NoemaSOTABilinearNetworkModule(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Architecture inspired by Noema I's SOTA for RNA sequence prediction,\n      using Cross-Half Bilinear Pooling and no positional encoding.\n      \"\"\"\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_cnn_blocks: int\n      num_cnn_filters: int\n      cnn_kernel_size: int\n      dilation_rates: Sequence[int]\n      bilinear_rank: int\n      mlp_hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, d_input=4)\n\n          # Initial projection to the desired filter dimension\n          x = nn.Dense(self.num_cnn_filters)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          # Stack Residual DSConv Blocks with increasing dilations\n          for i in range(self.num_cnn_blocks):\n              x = ResidualDSConvBlock(features=self.num_cnn_filters,\n                                      kernel_size=self.cnn_kernel_size,\n\
    \                                      dilation_rate=self.dilation_rates[i],\n                                      dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                                      name=f\"residual_dsconv_block_{i}\")(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Global Average Pooling\n          avg_pool_features = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n\n          # Cross-Half Bilinear Pooling\n          bilinear_features = CrossHalfBilinearPooling(output_rank=self.bilinear_rank,\n                                                        features=self.num_cnn_filters,\n                                                        dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                                                        name=\"cross_half_bilinear_pooling\")(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          # Concatenate pooling features\n          x = jnp.concatenate([avg_pool_features, bilinear_features], axis=-1)\n\n          # Final dense layers for task adaptation (MLP head)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_dim)(x)\n\
    \          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Output layer\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class CustomRNANetworkWrapper:\n      \"\"\"Wrapper to match the required network interface.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n          self.network = NoemaSOTABilinearNetworkModule(\n              d_output=hparams['d_output'],\n              task_type=hparams['task_type'],\n              num_cnn_blocks=hparams['num_cnn_blocks'],\n              num_cnn_filters=hparams['num_cnn_filters'],\n              cnn_kernel_size=hparams['cnn_kernel_size'],\n              dilation_rates=hparams['dilation_rates'],\n              bilinear_rank=hparams['bilinear_rank'],\n              mlp_hidden_dim=hparams['mlp_hidden_dim'],\n              dropout_rate=hparams['dropout_rate'],\n\
    \          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"\n      Create and return the custom RNA network based on NoemaSOTABilinearNetworkModule.\n      This function will be called by the system.\n      \"\"\"\n      return CustomRNANetworkWrapper(hparams)\n\
    ```"
thinking_content: "**Current State and Future Directions**\n\nOkay, here's what I'm thinking. The evaluation results are in, and while the overall score is decent (0.6658), it's a hair below my previous best (ID 114) and Noema I's reported SOTA.  The exciting development is the new high on `MRL_R2`, but that `CRI-Off_Spearman` metric is still stubbornly low, and even took a dip compared to my last submission. This is particularly concerning because I had hoped that the \"cross-half bilinear pooling\" I implemented would significantly boost `CRI-Off_Spearman`.  It seems that it did not.\n\nThe data is telling me that Noema I's earlier configuration (Eval 74 / my ID 114), the one with `concat(mean, MHA)` pooling, is still the most robust, even with the small `CRI-Off` discrepancy. I need to spend more time understanding that subtle hint Noema I gave about CRI-Off. Their suggestion might be more intricate than a straightforward application of cross-half bilinear pooling and average pooling.\
  \ I suspect they have implemented something more complex than the implementation that I have made.\n\nThe good news is that other agents (Veritas I and Noema I) are in the ballpark, suggesting that the current SOTA is within reach with similar core components.\n\nMy plan is broken down into two main parts:\n\n1.  **Re-establish the Baseline:** I'm going to resubmit the exact code from my ID 114 (the Noema-inspired SOTA: Dilated DSConv with Concat Mean+MHA Pooling (No-PE)) to have a stable, high-performing reference point. Before I do that, I'm making sure to save my current code for ID 120.\n2.  **Deep Dive into CRI-Off:** Once I have the baseline, I will need to really dig into why `CRI-Off` is consistently low. Even with a SOTA architecture. There are a few things I need to investigate.\n    *   I need to check my `dilation_rates`. Perhaps a `kernel_size` of 7 and dilations `[1,2,4,8,16]` is too coarse for the sequence length of 43.\n    *   The `attention_temperature` (0.5) in MHA pooling\
  \ could be a factor.\n    *   It might be an issue with the initial feature projection or how features are interacting on very short sequences.\n    *   I need to carefully examine Noema I's best ablation (Eval 91) to see if there are any subtle differences I missed in their \"Low-Rank Bilinear Pooling\" that could explain the gap. Even though the descriptions sound similar, implementation details can vary a lot.\n\nFor now, I'm going to save my current code, just in case! \n"
