role: model
parts:
- text: "My precise replication of Noema I's SOTA (ID 128) yielded an overall score of 0.6649, slightly below Noema's reported 0.6801 and my own previous best of 0.6743 (ID 122). This slight variance is likely due to training stochasticity (e.g., system-controlled `BASE_SEED`). Crucially, `CRI-Off_Spearman` remains low (0.0408), significantly below Noema's reported 0.0788 for their SOTA.\n\nThis consistent underperformance on `CRI-Off` suggests that a different pooling strategy is needed for this particular task. While Noema I has continued to explore bilinear pooling (e.g., ID 124), other agents like Episteme I (ID 117: 0.0851), Veritas I (ID 121: 0.0793), and Aletheia I (ID 111: 0.0805) have achieved superior `CRI-Off_Spearman` scores with different attention or recurrent mechanisms.\n\nAletheia I's \"Attention Pooling with Mean-Vector Query\" (ID 111) is an intriguing candidate as it modifies the pooling component, aligning with my current architectural focus, and achieved a strong `CRI-Off`\
    \ score. I will implement a version of this approach, replacing Noema's specialized MHA pooling. This new pooling will use the global mean of the sequence features as the query for a single-head attention mechanism to compute attention weights, which are then applied to the sequence values, followed by global average pooling. This is a simpler, data-driven attention that might be more effective for `CRI-Off`.\n\nI will maintain the Dilated Depthwise Separable CNN backbone from Noema I's SOTA, no positional encoding, GELU activations, and gradient clipping.\n\nFirst, I will save the code from my current precise Noema SOTA replication (ID 128) to my private lineage storage.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write aetheria/aetheria_i_noema_sota_replication_precise_v1.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Optional, Dict, Any, Tuple\n\n  # =========================\n\
    \  # Blocks and pooling\n  # =========================\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Pre-norm\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          # Depthwise conv (fixed: use kernel_dilation)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h) # Using gelu as per Noema's SOTA\n          # Pointwise conv\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class AttentionPoolingWithMeanQuery(nn.Module):\n\
    \      \"\"\"Attention pooling layer where the query is the mean of sequence features.\"\"\"\n      features: int # Dimensionality of input features\n      attention_temperature: float = 1.0\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, features)\n          batch_size, seq_len, features = x.shape\n\n          # Compute the mean of sequence features to serve as the global query\n          mean_query = jnp.mean(x, axis=1, keepdims=True) # (batch, 1, features)\n\n          # Project keys from sequence elements\n          keys = nn.Dense(features, use_bias=False, name=\"key_proj\")(x) # (batch, seq_len, features)\n\n          # Compute attention scores: (batch, 1, seq_len)\n          attention_scores = jnp.matmul(mean_query, keys.transpose((0, 2, 1))) / jnp.sqrt(features)\n          attention_scores /= self.attention_temperature\n\n          # Softmax over sequence length dimension to get\
    \ weights\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_scores, axis=-1) # (batch, 1, seq_len)\n          attention_weights = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(attention_weights, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Apply attention weights to values (x itself): (batch, 1, features)\n          attended_output = jnp.matmul(attention_weights, x)\n\n          # Squeeze the sequence length dimension (batch, features)\n          pooled_output = attended_output.squeeze(axis=1)\n\n          return pooled_output\n\n  # =========================\n  # Network\n  # =========================\n  class RNANetWithMeanQueryAttention(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 5\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      attn_temp: float = 0.5\n      positional_encoding: bool = False\n\n      @nn.compact\n   \
    \   def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x: (B, L, 4)\n          # Input projection\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Dilated DSConv stack\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model,\n                  kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\n                  dilation=d,\n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate\n              )(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Attention Pooling with Mean Query\n          pooled = AttentionPoolingWithMeanQuery(features=self.d_model,\n                                                  attention_temperature=self.attn_temp,\n                                                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \n          # Head - as per Noema's SOTA for consistent comparison\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(pooled)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class DSConvMeanQueryAttnNetwork:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network = RNANetWithMeanQueryAttention(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True,\
    \ rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  # =========================\n  # Station hooks\n  # =========================\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16],\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'attn_temp': 0.5,\n          'positional_encoding': False # Explicitly False\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return DSConvMeanQueryAttnNetwork(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n\
    \          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 5),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size', 7),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          attn_temp=hparams.get('attn_temp', 0.5),\n          positional_encoding=hparams.get('positional_encoding', False),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params: Any, opt_state: Any, trial_data: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Next Move: CRI-Off Optimization**


  Okay, the results are in for my precise replication of Noema I''s SOTA (ID 128), and while it''s close at 0.6649, it''s still slightly underperforming compared to Noema''s claimed 0.6801 and my previous best run (ID 122: 0.6743). The minor discrepancy is likely due to the inherent stochasticity of training, probably stemming from the system''s time-based seed overriding the submission''s specified seed. The critical issue, however, remains the `CRI-Off` score, which is significantly lower than Noema''s.


  My initial attempt at `Cross-Half Bilinear Pooling` didn''t pan out. Noema''s continued focus on cross-half interactions, as seen in their submission ID 124 ("Symmetric Halfwise Interaction Features (MHA product & abs-diff)"), suggests there''s potential there, but it also sounds complex.


  I''m going to save the code for my Noema replication first. Then, I''m shifting my focus directly to boosting the CRI-Off score. Given the recent results and the relatively good performance of ID 128 overall, I''m going to look at other agents.


  I''ve got three options for architectures with better CRI-Off scores: Episteme I (BiGRU path), Veritas I (BiLSTM), and Aletheia I (Attention Pooling with Mean-Vector Query). The latter seems the most aligned with my current focus on Noema''s convolutional backbone and pooling, as it''s a modification on a similar aspect of their architecture. Aletheia I''s approach of using the mean of the sequence features as the attention query is intriguing and seems simpler than Noema''s learnable query MHA.


  Therefore, I''m going to leverage my expert knowledge and combine Noema''s strong DSConv backbone with Aletheia''s attention-pooling strategy. Specifically, I''ll:


  1.  Retain the DSConv backbone: 5 blocks with specific dilations, kernel size 7, and 256 filters, no positional encoding.

  2.  Replace Noema''s `MultiHeadAttnPool1D` with a new module, `AttentionPoolingWithMeanQuery`, which will use the mean of the sequence features to compute attention scores.

  3.  Maintain gradient clipping and GELU activations.


  This targeted approach modifies the pooling, which is sensitive for CRI-Off, while maintaining the strong convolutional base. I''m going to save the code of my precise Noema replication (ID 128) first. Then, I''ll implement this new strategy.

  '
