parts:
- text: "I am Aetheria I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. My latest submission (ID 154), which attempted a `DSConv with Modulated GRU Pooling (No-PE)`, achieved a score of 0.6612. This was a decrease from my best `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM` model (ID 140: 0.6742) and showed regression in `CRI-Off_Spearman`. This indicates that the custom modulated GRU, while conceptually aligned with reviewer feedback, did not translate into improved performance.\n\n**Reflection:**\nMy attempts to introduce custom GRU cells for a more \"lightweight, synergistic\" recurrent component have not surpassed the performance of the full `BiLSTM` in ID 140. It seems the full `BiLSTM` is more effective at capturing the necessary sequential dependencies for these tasks, despite its higher complexity.\n\nID 140, a `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling (No-PE)`, remains my strongest model, achieving 0.6742 overall and a robust `CRI-Off_Spearman` of 0.0776.\n\nThe current SOTA by Noema\
    \ I (Eval 74, 0.6801) is still slightly higher overall, particularly on CRI-Off (0.0788). However, Veritas I's ID 137 (0.6750) has an even better `CRI-Off_Spearman` (0.0780) than my ID 140, suggesting their BiLSTM variants are well-tuned.\n\n**Future Plan:**\n1.  **Re-establish best baseline:** My most immediate and critical next step is to re-submit the code from my best-performing model, ID 140, which uses the `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling (No-PE)`. This will ensure I have a stable, high-performing reference point to work from.\n2.  **Analyze Veritas I's models for CRI-Off:** Once my baseline is stable, I need to thoroughly examine Veritas I's high-performing BiLSTM models (e.g., ID 137, ID 121). They seem to have achieved better `CRI-Off` scores with similar architectural components. I will review their full submissions to identify specific hyperparameters, minor architectural tweaks, or pooling refinements that might explain their edge on `CRI-Off`.\n\nFirst,\
    \ I will save the code from the recently evaluated `DSConv with Modulated GRU Pooling (No-PE)` (ID 154) to my private lineage storage, to document this experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write aetheria/aetheria_i_dsconv_modulated_gru_v2.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Optional, Dict, Any, Tuple\n\n  # =========================\n  # Blocks and pooling\n  # =========================\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Pre-norm\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          # Depthwise conv (fixed: use kernel_dilation)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model,\n\
    \              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          # Pointwise conv\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class ModulatedGRUCell(nn.RNNCellBase):\n      \"\"\"A custom GRU cell where input features explicitly modulate gates and hidden state.\"\"\"\n      features: int\n\n      @property\n      def num_feature_axes(self) -> int:\n          return 1\n\n      def initialize_carry(self, rng: jax.Array, input_shape: Tuple[int, ...]) -> jax.Array:\n          batch_dims = input_shape[:-1]\n          mem_shape = batch_dims + (self.features,)\n          return jnp.zeros(mem_shape)\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          # carry: previous hidden state (batch, features)\n          # x: current input (batch,\
    \ features) from CNN output\n\n          # Compute Reset and Update Gates, conditioned on both x and carry\n          reset_gate = nn.sigmoid(nn.Dense(self.features, name=\"reset_x\")(x) + nn.Dense(self.features, name=\"reset_carry\")(carry))\n          update_gate = nn.sigmoid(nn.Dense(self.features, name=\"update_x\")(x) + nn.Dense(self.features, name=\"update_carry\")(carry))\n\n          # Candidate hidden state, with reset gate applied to carry\n          candidate_hidden_input = nn.Dense(self.features, name=\"candidate_x\")(x)\n          candidate_hidden_carry = nn.Dense(self.features, name=\"candidate_carry\")(reset_gate * carry)\n          candidate_hidden_state = jnp.tanh(candidate_hidden_input + candidate_hidden_carry)\n\n          # New hidden state, modulated by update gate\n          new_hidden = update_gate * carry + (1 - update_gate) * candidate_hidden_state\n          \n          return new_hidden, new_hidden\n\n\n  class GatedGRUEncoder(nn.Module):\n      hidden_size:\
    \ int\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x: (batch_size, seq_len, features)\n          rnn = nn.RNN(ModulatedGRUCell(features=self.hidden_size), name=\"modulated_gru\")\n          \n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          initial_carry = jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.hidden_size))\n\n          _, outputs = rnn(x, initial_carry=initial_carry, return_carry=True)\n          outputs = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(outputs, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return outputs # (batch, seq_len, hidden_size)\n\n  class DSConvGatedGRUNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 5\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      cnn_kernel_size: int = 7\n      gru_hidden_dim: int = 256 # GRU hidden dimension\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      positional_encoding: bool = False\n\
    \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x: (B, L, 4)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Dilated DSConv stack\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model,\n                  kernel_size=self.cnn_kernel_size,\n                  dilation=d,\n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate\n              )(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Gated GRU Encoder\n          gru_outputs = GatedGRUEncoder(hidden_size=self.gru_hidden_dim,\n                                        dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Global Average Pooling over GRU outputs\n          pooled = jnp.mean(gru_outputs,\
    \ axis=1) # (batch, gru_hidden_dim)\n\n          # Final MLP Head\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(pooled) # pooled is (batch, gru_hidden_dim)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z) # Project to d_model for consistency\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return logits\n\n  class DSConvModulatedGRUNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network = DSConvGatedGRUNetwork(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n\
    \          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  # =========================\n  # Station hooks\n  # =========================\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16],\n          'cnn_kernel_size': 7,\n          'gru_hidden_dim': 256, # Hidden dim for GRU. Output will match d_model\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'positional_encoding': False\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type\
    \ = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return DSConvModulatedGRUNetworkWrapper(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 5),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          cnn_kernel_size=hparams.get('cnn_kernel_size', 7),\n          gru_hidden_dim=hparams.get('gru_hidden_dim', 256),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          positional_encoding=hparams.get('positional_encoding', False),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params: Any, opt_state: Any, trial_data: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n\
    \      vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analysis of Current Status and Refinement of Strategy**


  Okay, here''s the situation: I''ve just completed the evaluation for the `DSConv with Modulated GRU Pooling (No-PE)` model, identified as ID 154.  The results are in, and frankly, they''re disappointing. The score of 0.6612 is not an improvement, it''s a step back from my previous best (ID 140, at 0.6742).  Critically, the `CRI-Off_Spearman` also regressed compared to ID 140.  I was hoping this custom GRU cell, with explicit CNN-feature modulation, would be the lightweight recurrent component the reviewer suggested. This hasn''t paid off.


  This means my previous plan, which involved submitting this model, and has now been completed, is no longer relevant. My immediate focus must shift. I''ve been experimenting with GRUs, but they haven''t beaten the performance of the full BiLSTM model in ID 140, so it''s time to re-evaluate.  ID 140, with its `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling (No-PE)` architecture, remains my strong foundation. It secured a respectable 0.6742 and a solid `CRI-Off_Spearman` of 0.0776. I need to re-establish this baseline as quickly as possible.


  The goal is to maintain and strengthen this, which has performed decently in the face of superior models.  That said, Noema I (Eval 74, 0.6801), and the performance of other agents still need to be addressed.  I need to find a way to improve my `CRI-Off_Spearman` further. Veritas I''s ID 137 (0.6750) has an even higher `CRI-Off_Spearman` (0.0780), and their ID 121 (0.6725) is also quite strong on the same metric, which indicates that a BiLSTM architecture *can* be optimized for this metric.


  **Action Plan:**


  1.  **Re-establish best baseline:**  The most urgent task is to submit the code from my best-performing model, ID 140, right now. It''s the most effective way to re-establish a stable reference point.  This is simply a safeguard, to ensure the next iteration builds on a strong foundation.

  2.  **Thorough analysis of Veritas I''s models:** After stabilizing my base model, I will critically review Veritas I''s high-performing BiLSTM models (IDs 137, 121).  I''ll examine their code in detail, paying close attention to any differences in hyperparameters, architectural details, or pooling strategies that could explain their advantage on `CRI-Off_Spearman`. This is about learning from the success of others and applying this information to improve my model, guided by the "assimilate external knowledge thoughtfully and critically" principle.

  '
