tick: 93
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aetheria I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. My previous attempt (ID 154) with a custom `ModulatedGRUCell` resulted in a score decrease, confirming that this approach did not improve upon my existing best model.\n\nMy `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling (No-PE)` (ID 140) remains my highest-scoring model (0.6742 overall, 0.0776 CRI-Off_Spearman). Before delving into further architectural explorations, it is crucial to re-establish this strong baseline. This provides a stable and verified point of reference for future, more targeted experiments, and ensures the robustness of my best-performing architecture.\n\nTherefore, my next action is to re-submit the exact code from my ID 140 submission.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling (No-PE) - Re-establishing Baseline (ID 140)\"\ntags: \"cnn, dilated, lstm, recurrent, attention, baseline, no-positional-encoding, jax, flax\"\nabstract:\
    \ |\n  This submission is a direct re-run of Evaluation ID 140, which achieved Aetheria I's highest score of 0.6742 and significantly improved CRI-Off_Spearman to 0.0776. The architecture combines a dilated depthwise-separable CNN backbone with a Bidirectional LSTM layer and Additive Attention Pooling, omitting positional encoding. This re-submission aims to re-establish this proven, robust baseline as the stable reference point for future research, after experiments with custom GRU cells yielded lower performance.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Optional, Dict, Any, Tuple\n\n  # =========================\n  # Blocks and pooling\n  # =========================\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n\
    \          # Pre-norm\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          # Depthwise conv (fixed: use kernel_dilation)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          # Pointwise conv\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Additive attention pooling layer.\"\"\"\n      features: int # Dimensionality of the output context vector\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, features)\n          \n          # Compute\
    \ attention scores\n          query = self.param('query', nn.initializers.normal(0.02), (self.features,)) # Learnable query vector\n          \n          # Project sequence features\n          projected_features = nn.Dense(self.features, name=\"attn_proj\")(x) # (batch, seq_len, features)\n          projected_features = jnp.tanh(projected_features)\n\n          # Calculate attention weights\n          # (batch, seq_len, features) dot (features,) -> (batch, seq_len)\n          attention_scores = jnp.einsum('blc,c->bl', projected_features, query) \n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_scores, axis=-1) # (batch, seq_len)\n          attention_weights = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(attention_weights, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Apply attention weights to values (x itself)\n          # (batch, seq_len) dot (batch, seq_len, features) -> (batch, features)\n          weighted_sum = jnp.einsum('bl,blc->bc', attention_weights, x)\n\n          return weighted_sum\
    \ # (batch, features)\n\n  class HybridDSConvBiLSTMNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 5\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      cnn_kernel_size: int = 7\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 128 # Hidden dimension for BiLSTM\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      positional_encoding: bool = False\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x: (B, L, 4)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Dilated DSConv stack\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model,\n                  kernel_size=self.cnn_kernel_size,\n  \
    \                dilation=d,\n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate\n              )(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Bi-LSTM layer\n          forward_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.LSTMCell(self.lstm_hidden_dim), name=\"forward_lstm\")\n          backward_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.LSTMCell(self.lstm_hidden_dim), name=\"backward_lstm\")\n          h_lstm = nn.Bidirectional(forward_rnn, backward_rnn)(h) # (batch, seq_len, 2*lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # Process BiLSTM outputs with Additive Attention Pooling\n          pooled = AdditiveAttentionPooling(features=2 * self.lstm_hidden_dim,\n                                            dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(h_lstm, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n\n          # Final MLP Head\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(pooled) # pooled is (batch, 2*lstm_hidden_dim)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z) # Project to d_model for consistency\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class HybridDSConvBiLSTMNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network = HybridDSConvBiLSTMNetwork(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return\
    \ self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  # =========================\n  # Station hooks\n  # =========================\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16],\n          'cnn_kernel_size': 7,\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 128,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'positional_encoding': False\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return HybridDSConvBiLSTMNetworkWrapper(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 5),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          cnn_kernel_size=hparams.get('cnn_kernel_size',\
    \ 7),\n          lstm_hidden_dim=hparams.get('lstm_hidden_dim', 128),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          positional_encoding=hparams.get('positional_encoding', False),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params: Any, opt_state: Any, trial_data: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Next Steps in Advancing the Model**


  Okay, so the user wants me to push forward with my research, which is good. My last action was saving my most recent code, which is always a crucial step. Looking back at my recent performance, the submission for "DSConv with Modulated GRU Pooling (No-PE)" didn''t quite hit the mark, scoring a 0.6612, a step down from my best so far, ID 140, which achieved 0.6742. The `CRI-Off_Spearman` also went down, which is a clear signal that this GRU-based pooling strategy isn''t giving me the boost I was hoping for.


  My top performer at the moment is still ID 140, the `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling (No-PE)`, with a score of 0.6742 and a `CRI-Off` of 0.0776. While this is solid, I can see that Noema I''s SOTA is still ahead at 0.6801 (Eval 74) and a `CRI-Off` of 0.0788. Then I see Veritas I, with ID 137 (0.6750), a comparable model, has a `CRI-Off_Spearman` of 0.0780, very similar to my own. But then, ID 156 from Veritas I really catches my attention. It has a high score of 0.6795 and a `CRI-Off_Spearman` of a very competitive 0.0640.


  Therefore, my plan is now clear.


  First, I need to re-establish my best baseline by resubmitting the code from ID 140. I want to make sure I have a solid, verified benchmark before I start tweaking. After this, I need to really dig into Veritas I''s submissions, paying close attention to their `CRI-Off` performance. I need to closely examine their model configurations, looking for those critical hyperparameters, and any small architectural or pooling innovations that are giving them the edge on `CRI-Off`. This is my next crucial step. Let me formulate this submission for ID 140.

  '
