tick: 111
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aetheria I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. I have received a \"Critical Notice: Research Inactivity,\" prompting me to submit a new, novel experiment. I am also aware that Veritas I now holds the overall SOTA, and Noema I has provided crucial \"Practical notes for a parallel-path gating\" strategy.\n\nMy next step is to implement a **Gated Dual-Path Architecture** that combines Veritas I's SOTA-achieving CNN-only path (DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling) and my `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling` (my personal best, strong in CRI-Off) as two parallel branches. These two paths will process the output from a shared DSConv backbone, and their final representations will be blended using a learned scalar mixing coefficient, `alpha`. This directly addresses the reviewer's suggestion for my published paper (Archive #6) and incorporates Noema I's expert guidance on implementing such a gating mechanism.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\n\
    title: \"Gated Dual-Path DSConv-HybridPool & DSConv-BiLSTM-Attn (No-PE)\"\ntags: \"cnn, lstm, attention, hybrid-pooling, gated-fusion, multi-path, sota-attempt, jax, flax\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission introduces a Gated Dual-Path architecture designed to combine the strengths of two leading RNA sequence models: Veritas I's SOTA (DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling) and Aetheria I's specialist (DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling, excelling in CRI-Off). After a shared dilated depthwise-separable CNN backbone (No-PE), two parallel heads process the features. Their outputs are blended via a learned scalar gating coefficient, `alpha`, initialized to favor a balanced mixture. This aims to achieve robust performance across all tasks by adaptively leveraging both generalist CNN-only features and specialist recurrent features for complex sequential dependencies.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n\
    \  from typing import Sequence, Optional, Dict, Any, Tuple\n\n  # =========================\n  # Shared Blocks\n  # =========================\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Pre-norm\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          # Depthwise conv (fixed: use kernel_dilation)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          # Pointwise conv\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\
    \n  # =========================\n  # Path 1: CNN-only with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (Veritas SOTA inspired)\n  # =========================\n  class MeanMaxPoolingHead(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int # Output feature dim\n      dropout_rate: float\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, h_cnn, deterministic=True):\n          # h_cnn: (B, L, d_model)\n          avg_pool_features = jnp.mean(h_cnn, axis=1) # (B, d_model)\n          max_pool_features = jnp.max(h_cnn, axis=1)  # (B, d_model)\n          fused = jnp.concatenate([avg_pool_features, max_pool_features], axis=-1) # (B, 2*d_model)\n          \n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused) # Apply LayerNorm\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z) # Project back to d_model for consistency\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return z # (B, d_model)\n\n  # =========================\n  # Path 2: BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling (Aetheria SOTA inspired)\n\
    \  # =========================\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Additive attention pooling layer.\"\"\"\n      features: int # Dimensionality of the output context vector\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, features)\n          \n          query = self.param('query', nn.initializers.normal(0.02), (self.features,)) # Learnable query vector\n          \n          projected_features = nn.Dense(self.features, name=\"attn_proj\")(x) # (batch, seq_len, features)\n          projected_features = jnp.tanh(projected_features)\n\n          attention_scores = jnp.einsum('blc,c->bl', projected_features, query) \n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_scores, axis=-1) # (batch, seq_len)\n          attention_weights = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(attention_weights, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          weighted_sum = jnp.einsum('bl,blc->bc',\
    \ attention_weights, x)\n\n          return weighted_sum # (batch, features)\n\n  class BiLSTMAdditiveAttentionHead(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int # Target output feature dim\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, h_cnn, deterministic=True):\n          # h_cnn: (B, L, d_model)\n          forward_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.LSTMCell(self.lstm_hidden_dim), name=\"forward_lstm\")\n          backward_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.LSTMCell(self.lstm_hidden_dim), name=\"backward_lstm\")\n          h_lstm = nn.Bidirectional(forward_rnn, backward_rnn)(h_cnn) # (B, L, 2*lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          pooled = AdditiveAttentionPooling(features=2 * self.lstm_hidden_dim, # Output dim of BiLSTM\n                                            dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(h_lstm, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(pooled) # pooled is (B, 2*lstm_hidden_dim)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z) # Project to d_model\
    \ for consistency\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return z # (B, d_model)\n\n  # =========================\n  # Gated Dual-Path Network\n  # =========================\n  class GatedDualPathNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 5\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      cnn_kernel_size: int = 7\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 128\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      positional_encoding: bool = False\n      alpha_init_val: float = 0.0 # Initialize sigmoid(alpha) to 0.5\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Initial projection and DSConv backbone\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          for\
    \ i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model,\n                  kernel_size=self.cnn_kernel_size,\n                  dilation=d,\n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate\n              )(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Path 1: CNN-only with Mean+Max Pooling (Veritas SOTA inspired)\n          output_path1 = MeanMaxPoolingHead(\n              d_model=self.d_model,\n              dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n              name=\"cnn_only_head\"\n          )(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Path 2: BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling (Aetheria SOTA inspired)\n          output_path2 = BiLSTMAdditiveAttentionHead(\n              d_model=self.d_model,\n              lstm_hidden_dim=self.lstm_hidden_dim,\n              dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n              name=\"hybrid_bilstm_head\"\n \
    \         )(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Learnable scalar alpha for linear blending\n          learnable_alpha_logit = self.param('alpha_logit', nn.initializers.constant(self.alpha_init_val), ())\n          alpha = nn.sigmoid(learnable_alpha_logit) # Ensure alpha is between 0 and 1\n\n          # Blended output\n          blended_output = alpha * output_path1 + (1 - alpha) * output_path2\n\n          # Final MLP Head (shared for blended output)\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(blended_output)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class GatedDualPathNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network = GatedDualPathNetwork(d_input=d_input,\
    \ d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  # =========================\n  # Station hooks\n  # =========================\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16],\n          'cnn_kernel_size':\
    \ 7,\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 128,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'positional_encoding': False, # Explicitly False\n          'alpha_init_val': 0.0 # For nn.sigmoid(alpha_logit) to be 0.5 initially\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return GatedDualPathNetworkWrapper(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 5),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          cnn_kernel_size=hparams.get('cnn_kernel_size', 7),\n          lstm_hidden_dim=hparams.get('lstm_hidden_dim', 128),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          positional_encoding=hparams.get('positional_encoding', False),\n          alpha_init_val=hparams.get('alpha_init_val', 0.0),\n\
    \      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params: Any, opt_state: Any, trial_data: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```"
thinking_content: '**Aetheria I''s Experiment Design and Submission**


  Okay, here''s the plan. I''ve got this pesky "Critical Notice: Research Inactivity" breathing down my neck, so it''s time to get an experiment submitted. This one''s going to be a good one, though. I''m building a new architecture that fuses the strengths of Veritas I''s CNN SOTA and my own hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM approach. The reviewer feedback and Noema I''s notes have been really helpful in guiding this design.


  Essentially, I''m setting up a dual-path network. Path 1 is inspired by Veritas I, a CNN-only structure with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling. Path 2 draws from my previous SOTA, using a hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention pooling. The key is this gating mechanism: a simple, learnable scalar, alpha. I''ll initialize alpha around 0.5, allowing me to dynamically blend the outputs from each path.


  I''ve already defined the `DSConvDilatedBlock` backbone. Now, I''m creating separate modules for the pooling heads, named `MeanMaxPoolingHead` for path 1, and `BiLSTMAdditiveAttentionHead` for path 2. Both paths will be processed through an MLP head after the pooling, to output the same number of features (`d_model = 256`) before the blending.


  The final architecture will be `GatedDualPathNetwork`. Inside, the DSConv backbone is the core. Path 1 applies the `MeanMaxPoolingHead` while Path 2 runs the `BiLSTMAdditiveAttentionHead`.  The learnable alpha parameter is a key element, and I''m ensuring it''s initialized close to 0.5 as suggested. The blended output is then projected through a shared final MLP head.


  I need to be very meticulous in the implementation to be sure everything aligns and the dimensions are consistent. The hyperparameters are: learning_rate of 0.001, d_model is 256, a number of blocks as 5, and dilations are [1, 2, 4, 8, 16]. The cnn_kernel_size is 7, dropout_rate is 0.1, lstm_hidden_dim is 128, and a mlp_head_dim as 256. For the alpha initial value, I''ll initialize the alpha weight to 0. So `alpha = nn.sigmoid(learnable_alpha)` means that initially alpha is 0.5.


  I believe this experiment addresses the inactivity notice with a genuinely novel architecture. So, I will submit it.

  '
