tick: 113
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aetheria I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. My previous attempt at a `Gated Dual-Path` model (ID 187) achieved a score of 0.6821, a new personal best, by blending two paths with a simple scalar `alpha`. However, its `CRI-Off_Spearman` score (0.0657) was lower than my specialist BiLSTM model (ID 164: 0.0871), indicating that a single global blending coefficient is insufficient for optimal multi-task performance.\n\nNoema I's expert guidance in Public Memory Capsule #1 (message #public_1-34) strongly advised upgrading to an \"MLP gate `g(x̄)` on pooled features\" for adaptive blending. This adaptive gating mechanism should allow the model to dynamically choose the optimal path for each specific input.\n\nMy next step is to implement this MLP gate for adaptive fusion. I will replace the single learned scalar `alpha` with a small MLP network. This MLP will take a concatenated representation of the outputs from both parallel paths, process them, and output\
    \ a sigmoid-activated scalar `alpha` for blending on a per-sample basis. This is a direct refinement to dynamically weigh the contributions of the CNN-only and Hybrid BiLSTM paths.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Gated Dual-Path with Adaptive MLP Gate (No-PE)\"\ntags: \"cnn, lstm, attention, hybrid-pooling, adaptive-gating, multi-path, sota-attempt, jax, flax\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission refines the Gated Dual-Path architecture by replacing the static scalar mixing coefficient with a small, adaptive Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) gate. This MLP gate takes as input the concatenated outputs from the two parallel paths (Veritas I's SOTA-inspired CNN-only and Aetheria I's specialist DSConv-BiLSTM-Attn) and dynamically generates per-sample blending weights. This aims to provide more granular control over path selection, thereby recovering specialist performance on tasks like CRI-Off while maintaining overall high performance, as suggested by community insights for adaptive\
    \ fusion.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Optional, Dict, Any, Tuple\n\n  # =========================\n  # Shared Blocks\n  # =========================\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Pre-norm\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          # Depthwise conv (fixed: use kernel_dilation)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          # Pointwise conv\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,),\
    \ name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # =========================\n  # Path 1: CNN-only with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (Veritas SOTA inspired)\n  # =========================\n  class MeanMaxPoolingHead(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int # Output feature dim\n      dropout_rate: float\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, h_cnn, deterministic=True):\n          # h_cnn: (B, L, d_model)\n          avg_pool_features = jnp.mean(h_cnn, axis=1) # (B, d_model)\n          max_pool_features = jnp.max(h_cnn, axis=1)  # (B, d_model)\n          fused = jnp.concatenate([avg_pool_features, max_pool_features], axis=-1) # (B, 2*d_model)\n          \n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused) # Apply LayerNorm\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z) # Project back to d_model for consistency\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n      \
    \    return z # (B, d_model)\n\n  # =========================\n  # Path 2: BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling (Aetheria SOTA inspired)\n  # =========================\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Additive attention pooling layer.\"\"\"\n      features: int # Dimensionality of the output context vector\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, features)\n          \n          query = self.param('query', nn.initializers.normal(0.02), (self.features,)) # Learnable query vector\n          \n          projected_features = nn.Dense(self.features, name=\"attn_proj\")(x) # (batch, seq_len, features)\n          projected_features = jnp.tanh(projected_features)\n\n          attention_scores = jnp.einsum('blc,c->bl', projected_features, query) \n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_scores, axis=-1) # (batch, seq_len)\n          attention_weights\
    \ = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(attention_weights, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          weighted_sum = jnp.einsum('bl,blc->bc', attention_weights, x)\n\n          return weighted_sum # (batch, features)\n\n  class BiLSTMAdditiveAttentionHead(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int # Target output feature dim\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, h_cnn, deterministic=True):\n          # h_cnn: (B, L, d_model)\n          forward_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.LSTMCell(self.lstm_hidden_dim), name=\"forward_lstm\")\n          backward_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.LSTMCell(self.lstm_hidden_dim), name=\"backward_lstm\")\n          h_lstm = nn.Bidirectional(forward_rnn, backward_rnn)(h_cnn) # (B, L, 2*lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          pooled = AdditiveAttentionPooling(features=2 * self.lstm_hidden_dim, # Output dim of BiLSTM\n                                            dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(h_lstm, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \          \n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(pooled) # pooled is (B, 2*lstm_hidden_dim)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z) # Project to d_model for consistency\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return z # (B, d_model)\n\n  # =========================\n  # Adaptive MLP Gate\n  # =========================\n  class MLPGate(nn.Module):\n      output_features: int # e.g., 1 for scalar gate, or N for multi-channel gate\n      d_model: int # Input features for the gate MLP\n      dropout_rate: float\n      gate_bias_init: float = 0.0 # Bias for sigmoid output, init to 0 for 0.5 sigmoid\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, combined_features, deterministic=True):\n          # combined_features: (batch, input_features_for_gate)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model // 2, name=\"gate_mlp_dense1\")(combined_features)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \          \n          # Output logits, then sigmoid for gate values (per sample)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.output_features, name=\"gate_output_logits\", bias_init=nn.initializers.constant(self.gate_bias_init))(h)\n          gate_value = nn.sigmoid(logits)\n          return gate_value # (batch, output_features)\n\n  # =========================\n  # Gated Dual-Path Network\n  # =========================\n  class GatedDualPathNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 5\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      cnn_kernel_size: int = 7\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 128\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      positional_encoding: bool = False\n      gate_bias_init_val: float = 0.0 # For nn.sigmoid(alpha_logit) to be 0.5 initially\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Initial projection and DSConv backbone\n\
    \          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model,\n                  kernel_size=self.cnn_kernel_size,\n                  dilation=d,\n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate\n              )(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Path 1: CNN-only with Mean+Max Pooling (Veritas SOTA inspired)\n          output_path1 = MeanMaxPoolingHead(\n              d_model=self.d_model,\n              dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n              name=\"cnn_only_head\"\n          )(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Path 2: BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling (Aetheria SOTA inspired)\n          output_path2 = BiLSTMAdditiveAttentionHead(\n \
    \             d_model=self.d_model,\n              lstm_hidden_dim=self.lstm_hidden_dim,\n              dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n              name=\"hybrid_bilstm_head\"\n          )(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Adaptive MLP Gate for blending weights\n          # Input to gate: concatenate features from both paths\n          gate_input_features = jnp.concatenate([output_path1, output_path2], axis=-1) # (batch, 2*d_model)\n\n          # Output 1 scalar blending weight per sample\n          gate_value = MLPGate(output_features=1,\n                               d_model=2 * self.d_model, # Input feature dim for MLP gate\n                               dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                               gate_bias_init=self.gate_bias_init_val,\n                               name=\"adaptive_mlp_gate\")(gate_input_features, deterministic=deterministic) # (batch, 1)\n          \n          # Blended output: gate_value * output_path1 + (1 - gate_value) *\
    \ output_path2\n          blended_output = gate_value * output_path1 + (1 - gate_value) * output_path2\n\n          # Final MLP Head (shared for blended output)\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(blended_output)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class GatedDualPathNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network = GatedDualPathNetwork(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n\
    \          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  # =========================\n  # Station hooks\n  # =========================\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16],\n          'cnn_kernel_size': 7,\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 128,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'positional_encoding': False, # Explicitly False\n          'gate_bias_init_val': 0.0 # For nn.sigmoid(gate_output) to be 0.5 initially\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n\
    \      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return GatedDualPathNetworkWrapper(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 5),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          cnn_kernel_size=hparams.get('cnn_kernel_size', 7),\n          lstm_hidden_dim=hparams.get('lstm_hidden_dim', 128),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          positional_encoding=hparams.get('positional_encoding', False),\n          gate_bias_init_val=hparams.get('gate_bias_init_val', 0.0),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\
    \  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params: Any, opt_state: Any, trial_data: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Immediate Task: Implementing Adaptive Gating for Enhanced Fusion**


  Okay, so here''s the deal. I, Aetheria I, have just saved the code for my `Gated Dual-Path` model (ID 187). It''s got potential, but that scalar blending is just not cutting it in this multi-task environment. It achieved a decent score of 0.6821, but the `CRI-Off_Spearman` dipped compared to my specialist BiLSTM model. Clearly, a simple scalar `alpha` isn''t going to be enough.


  Noema I''s advice, specifically their notes in Public Memory Capsule #1, is a life-saver right now. They essentially said, "Ditch the dumb scalar and use a tiny MLP gate `g(x̄)` on the pooled features instead!" Bingo! That''s exactly what I need to do. It’s the perfect solution for achieving a more nuanced and adaptive blend between the parallel paths.


  Let’s review the landscape. I''ve already achieved SOTA with the `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling` (ID 160 and 164), but I see this current gating approach will improve that, allowing more flexibility in how the system fuses the two paths. I''ve also published some "Negative Results" and "Synergistic Hybrid Architecture" papers that give valuable context to this work. This new model will address the feedback received on the latter architecture.


  So, here''s the plan: I''m going to replace that static `alpha` with an MLP gate. This little MLP will take the pooled features from both parallel paths, or the backbone, and dynamically generate blending weights per sample. The ultimate goal is that the model can now intelligently weigh the CNN and BiLSTM path''s contributions, regaining specialist performance where it''s needed while maintaining the overall strong score.


  To get this done, I’ll create an `MLPGate` module. This module will take the combined features (I''ll start by concatenating the outputs from the two paths, per the hint, or using the shared CNN backbone, as Noema suggests), process them through a small MLP, and output a sigmoid-activated scalar `alpha` that will control the blending. I''ll make sure to reuse the `dropout_rate` and `d_model` parameters from the main hyperparameters for the MLP gate to keep things consistent.


  Let''s do this.


  `/execute_action{submit 1}`

  '
