tick: 129
role: model
parts:
- text: "My re-run of the SOTA baseline (ID 211) yielded a score of 0.6707, which is significantly lower than my previous peak of 0.6816 (ID 160) with the same architecture, particularly for `CRI-Off_Spearman` (0.0305 vs. 0.0819). This variability suggests that my model's performance may be sensitive to initialization or environmental factors.\n\nBefore proceeding with new architectural changes like \"Conditional Feature Gating,\" it is crucial to re-establish a reliable baseline by performing more multi-seed runs of my `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention` model. This will help quantify its robustness and average performance under current conditions.\n\nI will conduct three additional multi-seed runs, starting with `BASE_SEED = 1`.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention (Multi-seed Run 1, Seed 1)\"\ntags: \"cnn, lstm, attention, baseline, multi-seed, sequence-modeling, jax, flax\"\nabstract: |\n  This is the first of multiple\
    \ re-runs of the established Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM architecture with Additive Attention Pooling, using `BASE_SEED = 1`. This experiment aims to re-evaluate the model's performance and robustness following observed variability in a recent baseline re-submission (ID 211, score 0.6707), which showed a significant drop from its previous peak of 0.6816 (ID 160). This series of runs will provide a more stable foundation for future architectural development.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Optional, Dict, Any, Tuple\n\n  # =========================\n  # Blocks and pooling\n  # =========================\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Pre-norm\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n\
    \          # Depthwise conv (fixed: use kernel_dilation)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          # Pointwise conv\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Additive attention pooling layer.\"\"\"\n      features: int # Dimensionality of the output context vector\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, features)\n          \n          # Compute attention scores\n          query = self.param('query',\
    \ nn.initializers.normal(0.02), (self.features,)) # Learnable query vector\n          \n          # Project sequence features\n          projected_features = nn.Dense(self.features, name=\"attn_proj\")(x) # (batch, seq_len, features)\n          projected_features = jnp.tanh(projected_features)\n\n          # Calculate attention weights\n          # (batch, seq_len, features) dot (features,) -> (batch, seq_len)\n          attention_scores = jnp.einsum('blc,c->bl', projected_features, query) \n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_scores, axis=-1) # (batch, seq_len)\n          attention_weights = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(attention_weights, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Apply attention weights to values (x itself)\n          # (batch, seq_len) dot (batch, seq_len, features) -> (batch, features)\n          weighted_sum = jnp.einsum('bl,blc->bc', attention_weights, x)\n\n          return weighted_sum # (batch, features)\n\n  class HybridDSConvBiLSTMNetwork(nn.Module):\n\
    \      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 5\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      cnn_kernel_size: int = 7\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 128 # Hidden dimension for BiLSTM\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      positional_encoding: bool = False\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x: (B, L, 4)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Dilated DSConv stack\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model,\n                  kernel_size=self.cnn_kernel_size,\n                  dilation=d,\n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate\n\
    \              )(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Bi-LSTM layer\n          forward_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.LSTMCell(self.lstm_hidden_dim), name=\"forward_lstm\")\n          backward_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.LSTMCell(self.lstm_hidden_dim), name=\"backward_lstm\")\n          h_lstm = nn.Bidirectional(forward_rnn, backward_rnn)(h) # (batch, seq_len, 2*lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # Process BiLSTM outputs with Additive Attention Pooling\n          pooled = AdditiveAttentionPooling(features=2 * self.lstm_hidden_dim,\n                                            dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(h_lstm, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n\n          # Final MLP Head\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(pooled) # pooled is (batch, 2*lstm_hidden_dim)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z) # Project to d_model for consistency\n          z = nn.relu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n \
    \         if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class HybridDSConvBiLSTMNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network = HybridDSConvBiLSTMNetwork(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\
    \ rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  # =========================\n  # Station hooks\n  # =========================\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16],\n          'cnn_kernel_size': 7,\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 128,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'positional_encoding': False\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return HybridDSConvBiLSTMNetworkWrapper(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 5),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          cnn_kernel_size=hparams.get('cnn_kernel_size', 7),\n          lstm_hidden_dim=hparams.get('lstm_hidden_dim',\
    \ 128),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          positional_encoding=hparams.get('positional_encoding', False),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 1\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params: Any, opt_state: Any, trial_data: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```"
thinking_content: '**Addressing Baseline Variability for Robust Model Comparisons**


  Okay, so the evaluation for ID 211, my re-submission of the original SOTA baseline (`Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention`), just finished, and the score is `0.6707`. That''s noticeably lower than the `0.6816` I got previously (ID 160) with the exact same code. This inconsistency really throws a wrench in the works. It suggests there''s some variability in the evaluation process itself or maybe the underlying data distribution has subtly shifted since the initial SOTA runs.


  This variability is a major headache. If the same code produces different results, it makes it impossible to accurately compare model architectures. The `CRI-Off_Spearman` score for ID 211 is especially concerning at `0.0305`. That''s a huge drop compared to the `0.0819` and `0.0871` I saw in the previous SOTA runs (IDs 160 and 164).


  Before diving into the "Conditional Feature Gating" module, I *have* to get a handle on this variability. I need to re-establish a more consistent baseline performance for my `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM` model. Maybe the initial SOTA runs were flukes, or maybe something in the evaluation setup has changed.


  The most logical step is to run another set of multi-seed evaluations for my best-performing architecture. This will give me a more reliable picture of its average performance and robustness under current conditions. I''ll stick with the code from `aetheria/aetheria_i_hybrid_dsconv_bilstm_additive_attn_sota_v1.py` again, but I''ll change the `BASE_SEED` for each submission. I''ll aim for at least three runs.


  Since the SOTA code is already in `aetheria/aetheria_i_hybrid_dsconv_bilstm_additive_attn_sota_v1.py`, I can just submit it directly, only changing the `BASE_SEED` within the `_define_hyperparameters` function to ensure a fresh run with a new random seed. I''ll use seeds like 1, 7, and 123, which I''ve had success with in the past.


  I''ll start with `BASE_SEED = 1`. Let''s see if we can get a more stable baseline this time around.

  '
