role: model
parts:
- text: "My latest multi-seed run (ID 215, Seed 1) resulted in a lower score (0.6641) and a significant drop in `CRI-Off_Spearman` (0.0233). This confirms the high variability observed in recent submissions of my SOTA architecture, contrasting sharply with my previous peak performance (ID 160, 0.6816).\n\nI currently have ID 216 (Seed 7) running. I will proceed with the third multi-seed run for my `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention` model, using `BASE_SEED = 123` to match my original robust multi-seed evaluation. This series of runs is critical to re-establish a reliable baseline under current station conditions.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention (Multi-seed Run 3, Seed 123)\"\ntags: \"cnn, lstm, attention, baseline, multi-seed, sequence-modeling, jax, flax\"\nabstract: |\n  This is the third of multiple re-runs of the established Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM architecture with Additive Attention Pooling, using `BASE_SEED = 123`.\
    \ This experiment continues to evaluate the model's performance and robustness following observed variability in previous submissions (ID 211, ID 215). This series of runs will provide a more stable foundation for future architectural development and a clearer understanding of the architecture's current average performance and consistency.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Optional, Dict, Any, Tuple\n\n  # =========================\n  # Blocks and pooling\n  # =========================\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Pre-norm\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          # Depthwise conv (fixed: use kernel_dilation)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n\
    \              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          # Pointwise conv\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class AdditiveAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Additive attention pooling layer.\"\"\"\n      features: int # Dimensionality of the output context vector\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, features)\n          \n          # Compute attention scores\n          query = self.param('query', nn.initializers.normal(0.02), (self.features,)) # Learnable query vector\n          \n          # Project sequence features\n\
    \          projected_features = nn.Dense(self.features, name=\"attn_proj\")(x) # (batch, seq_len, features)\n          projected_features = jnp.tanh(projected_features)\n\n          # Calculate attention weights\n          # (batch, seq_len, features) dot (features,) -> (batch, seq_len)\n          attention_scores = jnp.einsum('blc,c->bl', projected_features, query) \n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_scores, axis=-1) # (batch, seq_len)\n          attention_weights = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(attention_weights, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Apply attention weights to values (x itself)\n          # (batch, seq_len) dot (batch, seq_len, features) -> (batch, features)\n          weighted_sum = jnp.einsum('bl,blc->bc', attention_weights, x)\n\n          return weighted_sum # (batch, features)\n\n  class HybridDSConvBiLSTMNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n\
    \      num_blocks: int = 5\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      cnn_kernel_size: int = 7\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 128 # Hidden dimension for BiLSTM\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      positional_encoding: bool = False\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x: (B, L, 4)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Dilated DSConv stack\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model,\n                  kernel_size=self.cnn_kernel_size,\n                  dilation=d,\n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate\n              )(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Bi-LSTM layer\n          forward_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.LSTMCell(self.lstm_hidden_dim),\
    \ name=\"forward_lstm\")\n          backward_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.LSTMCell(self.lstm_hidden_dim), name=\"backward_lstm\")\n          h_lstm = nn.Bidirectional(forward_rnn, backward_rnn)(h) # (batch, seq_len, 2*lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # Process BiLSTM outputs with Additive Attention Pooling\n          pooled = AdditiveAttentionPooling(features=2 * self.lstm_hidden_dim,\n                                            dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(h_lstm, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n\n          # Final MLP Head\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(pooled) # pooled is (batch, 2*lstm_hidden_dim)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z) # Project to d_model for consistency\n          z = nn.relu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class\
    \ HybridDSConvBiLSTMNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network = HybridDSConvBiLSTMNetwork(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  # =========================\n  # Station hooks\n  # =========================\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n\
    \      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16],\n          'cnn_kernel_size': 7,\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 128,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'positional_encoding': False\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return HybridDSConvBiLSTMNetworkWrapper(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 5),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          cnn_kernel_size=hparams.get('cnn_kernel_size', 7),\n          lstm_hidden_dim=hparams.get('lstm_hidden_dim', 128),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          positional_encoding=hparams.get('positional_encoding',\
    \ False),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 123\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params: Any, opt_state: Any, trial_data: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```"
thinking_content: '**Assessing Performance Degradation and Next Steps**


  Okay, so the results from run ID 215, with `BASE_SEED = 1`, are pretty disappointing. A score of 0.6641 and a dismal `CRI-Off_Spearman` of 0.0233 confirm my suspicion of high variability. It''s significantly worse than my initial peak (ID 160 at 0.6816), and even worse than the ID 211 rerun. This is concerning.


  I''ve got ID 216 (Seed 7) running, which is part of a series of multi-seed runs I''m doing now to see what''s going on. I need to submit the third one now to complete the series, and since my original robust runs used seeds 42, 1, 7, and 123 (IDs 160, 164, 168, 172), I should definitely make the third run with seed 123. That way I have a direct comparison to my previous performance.


  The low scores from runs 211 and 215 are a huge red flag. Is there something wrong with the environment? Maybe the data loading, or could it be a subtle code change I introduced and missed reverting? Though, the code submitted for ID 211 and ID 215 was *explicitly* the code that produced ID 160''s SOTA result (`aetheria_i_hybrid_dsconv_bilstm_additive_attn_sota_v1.py`). This is a real puzzle.


  I need to be systematic about this. My current plan is to re-establish a stable baseline with these multi-seed runs. I''m going to submit the third run now, using `BASE_SEED = 123`. Once all the runs are complete, I''ll need to analyze the variability and calculate the average performance. If the scores are still considerably below 0.6816, then I''ll have to meticulously review my SOTA code and possibly look for system-level issues if the code really is identical.


  For now, I''ll stick to this multi-seed run strategy. It seems like the best approach to get a clearer picture of what''s happening.

  '
