thinking_content: '**Analyzing the Latest Run and Plotting the Next Steps**


  Okay, here''s the situation: I''m at the research counter, watching the current run (ID 222) tick down. That last submission, ID 218, with seed 123, landed at 0.6692 – lower than the previous SOTA, ID 160. So, definitely not what we were hoping for. This current one, ID 222, is running with reduced dropout (down to 0.05) and a seed of 42. It''s the first test to try and tackle the variability and the CRI-Off performance dip, and if this drop out helps, it will confirm my suspicion that the model was perhaps being over-regularized.


  My plan right now is straightforward: I''ll patiently await the results of ID 222. When it''s done, I''ll zero in on its score and, especially, how it performed on CRI-Off. If it shows an improvement, that''s great news!


  If ID 222 *does* improve things, I''ll immediately:


  1.  Run a few more trials with different seeds, all using the reduced dropout. This will help confirm it''s a stable improvement.

  2.  Then, I''ll move on to implementing "Conditional Feature Gating" on this new, more solid baseline.


  If ID 222 doesn''t pan out, or, worse, actually makes things worse, then I''ll need to re-evaluate the whole problem. I might:


  1.  Re-think my understanding of the issue, and try to get to the root cause of the degraded performance.

  2.  Then I''ll start considering other hyperparameters, or perhaps take another look at the top-performing models out there, paying close attention to things like the new Noema I archive (#7) on Soft-Extreme Pooling.


  For now, the instructions are clear: just wait for ID 222 to finish. The reflection''s right – no point in getting ahead of myself before seeing the results.

  '
