tick: 134
role: model
parts:
- text: "My latest experiment (ID 222), with a reduced `dropout_rate`, yielded an even lower score (0.6602) and critically low `CRI-Off_Spearman` (0.0276). This confirms that neither GELU activation nor reduced dropout effectively addressed the performance degradation and variability observed in my `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention` model. The current performance is significantly below my previous peak (ID 160: 0.6816).\n\nThis consistent degradation strongly suggests a deeper instability within the architecture or its training under current conditions, rather than a simple hyperparameter or activation issue. The persistent low `CRI-Off` score is particularly indicative of this.\n\nTo pinpoint the source of instability, I will perform an ablation study on the `AdditiveAttentionPooling` layer. My hypothesis is that this layer, or its interaction with the BiLSTM outputs, might be the cause of the instability, especially for tasks with short sequences like CRI-Off where attention\
    \ might struggle to learn meaningful weights.\n\nI will modify my SOTA architecture to replace `AdditiveAttentionPooling` with a simpler `Global Average Pooling`. This will allow me to check if a more basic pooling mechanism restores stability and better performance, thereby isolating the `AdditiveAttentionPooling` layer as a potential source of the problem. I will use `BASE_SEED = 42`.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Global Average Pooling (Ablation of Additive Attention)\"\ntags: \"cnn, lstm, pooling-ablation, global-average-pooling, sequence-modeling, jax, flax\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment performs an ablation study on the `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM` architecture by replacing the `AdditiveAttentionPooling` layer with a simple `Global Average Pooling`. The goal is to isolate the source of recent performance degradation and high variability, particularly for the CRI-Off task. If Global Average Pooling restores stability and improves CRI-Off\
    \ performance, it would suggest the `AdditiveAttentionPooling` layer or its specific implementation is contributing to instability under current training conditions. This aims to re-establish a stable baseline before further architectural innovations. Uses `BASE_SEED = 42`.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Optional, Dict, Any, Tuple\n\n  # =========================\n  # Blocks and pooling\n  # =========================\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Pre-norm\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          # Depthwise conv (fixed: use kernel_dilation)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n             \
    \ feature_group_count=self.d_model,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          # Pointwise conv\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # No AdditiveAttentionPooling class for this submission\n\n  class HybridDSConvBiLSTMNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 5\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      cnn_kernel_size: int = 7\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int = 128 # Hidden dimension for BiLSTM\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      positional_encoding: bool = False\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x: (B, L, 4)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model,\
    \ name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Dilated DSConv stack\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model,\n                  kernel_size=self.cnn_kernel_size,\n                  dilation=d,\n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate\n              )(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Bi-LSTM layer\n          forward_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.LSTMCell(self.lstm_hidden_dim), name=\"forward_lstm\")\n          backward_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.LSTMCell(self.lstm_hidden_dim), name=\"backward_lstm\")\n          h_lstm = nn.Bidirectional(forward_rnn, backward_rnn)(h) # (batch, seq_len, 2*lstm_hidden_dim)\n          \n          # MODIFIED: Replaced AdditiveAttentionPooling with Global Average Pooling\n          pooled = jnp.mean(h_lstm,\
    \ axis=1) # (batch, 2*lstm_hidden_dim)\n\n\n          # Final MLP Head\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(pooled) # pooled is (batch, 2*lstm_hidden_dim)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z) # Project to d_model for consistency\n          z = nn.relu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class HybridDSConvBiLSTMNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network = HybridDSConvBiLSTMNetwork(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n     \
    \     return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  # =========================\n  # Station hooks\n  # =========================\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16],\n          'cnn_kernel_size': 7,\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 128,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'positional_encoding': False\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n \
    \     return HybridDSConvBiLSTMNetworkWrapper(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 5),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          cnn_kernel_size=hparams.get('cnn_kernel_size', 7),\n          lstm_hidden_dim=hparams.get('lstm_hidden_dim', 128),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          positional_encoding=hparams.get('positional_encoding', False),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params: Any, opt_state: Any, trial_data: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n\
    \      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```"
thinking_content: '**Recent experiments have left me scratching my head.** My last run (ID 222), with a decreased dropout rate, somehow performed worse across the board, even compared to previous disappointing results. The `CRI-Off_Spearman` score is particularly troubling, indicating a serious issue with stability. The consistent underperformance of my `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention` model is a significant concern. I''m starting to think there''s a deeper problem than just tweaking hyperparameters.


  Given the inconsistency, I''m going to put the "Conditional Feature Gating" idea on hold.  It''s unwise to build upon a shaky foundation. I need to get back to basics. I''ve already tried messing with dropout and activation functions without success.  Maybe the problem is with optimization itself or the way the model components are interacting, especially during initialization or with certain data conditions.


  I''m also really puzzled because I got my best score of 0.6816 using `BASE_SEED = 42`. The recent runs *also* used `BASE_SEED = 42` but didn''t come close. Even runs with different seeds haven''t been good.


  I need to revisit the **`AdditiveAttentionPooling` layer** - especially how it''s talking to the BiLSTM outputs and how it might be causing this `CRI-Off` degradation. `CRI-Off` has really short sequences, and maybe my attention mechanism isn''t working well with them. It could be that the initial projection of the BiLSTM features is problematic.


  Looking at what others are doing, Noema I has a very strong `CRI-Off` score and is using a different pooling mechanism: `concat(mean, LSE_tau=0.5)`. Maybe that''s the key.


  My next step is to **replace `AdditiveAttentionPooling` with a simpler method: Global Average Pooling**, which has been stable in my CNN models in the past. If the model becomes more stable, it means that the attention mechanism is causing the variability.  If it''s still unstable, I''ll know there''s something wrong with the DSConv-BiLSTM combo.


  So, I''m going to take the code from my best-performing model, but I''m going to *completely* swap out the `AdditiveAttentionPooling` layer with `jnp.mean(h_lstm, axis=1)` to get the Global Average Pooling. I''ll use `BASE_SEED = 42` and see what happens.

  '
