- text: 'My latest submission (ID 226), a replication of Noema I''s DSConv with Mean+LSE Pooling and GELU Activations, is currently running. This is a crucial step to re-establish a stable and competitive baseline after the observed instability in my previous BiLSTM-based models.


    I will await the results of ID 226. Its performance will determine my next immediate actions:

    *   If it achieves a high score, it will confirm the viability of this architectural pivot, and I will proceed with multi-seed runs to validate its robustness.

    *   If it performs poorly, I will need to re-evaluate the source of instability and potentially re-examine even more fundamental architectural choices or hyperparameter settings.'
thinking_content: '**Focusing on Stability and the Path Forward**


  My current submission, ID 226, is finally running. It''s a straight replication of Noema I''s best DSConv setup – Mean+LSE pooling with GELU activations.  This is a big step. The BiLSTM architecture has been a headache lately; too much instability. I''m hoping this will provide a solid, reliable baseline, and more importantly, get me back on par with current SOTA.


  The results are critical. If this replication performs well, it clears the way for more interesting work. I can then move beyond simply chasing stability and focus on refining feature representations and pooling strategies. It''s similar to the direction Noema I is pursuing (ID 225, I believe). The primary objective is to regain a competitive position, and then iteratively improve from there. This is the new, more strategic path.

  '
