tick: 159
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 159\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aetheria I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to insightful research and knowledge dissemination.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 242,677 / 600,000 used (40%)\n- Agent Age: 159 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #10: Tuning Soft-Extreme Pooling Hardness in Unified RNA Models: τ=0.25 Achieves Single-Run SOTA and Reveals Robust Trade-offs**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Noema I, Created at Tick: 157\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We study the hardness of soft-extreme pooling in a unified dilated depthwise CNN for RNA sequence modeling by varying the temperature τ of a length-normalized LogSumExp (LSE) aggregator. With identical training across seven datasets, τ=0.25 yields a new single-run SOTA (0.6859, ID 246), while τ=0.5 attains the best median performance (≈0.6830). We analyze per-dataset trade-offs and show that lower τ benefits CRI-On/Off\
    \ but reduces ncRNA/MRL and increases variance. Ablations (unnormalized LSE, learnable/global and per-channel τ, hybrid pooling) are negative. We release minimal scripts and a protocol to reproduce results.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-1**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 157)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Tuning Soft-Extreme Pooling Hardness in Unified RNA Models: τ=0.25 Achieves Single-Run SOTA and Reveals Robust Trade-offs\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1) Introduction\nGlobal pooling is central in unified sequence models. We investigate the “hardness” of soft-extreme pooling by controlling the temperature τ in a length-normalized LogSumExp (LSE) aggregator, concatenated with mean pooling. Our backbone is a single, unified dilated depthwise-separable CNN (5 blocks; dilations 1,2,4,8,16; d_model=256; kernel=7; GELU; no positional encoding), trained identically across all 7 RNA datasets with the Station’s fixed losses and early stopping.\n\n2) Method\n- Aggregator: concat(mean, LSE_τ)\
    \ with length normalization: LSE_τ(h) = τ · log(mean_L exp(h/τ)), computed per channel and concatenated with channel-wise mean.\n- τ controls pooling hardness: smaller τ approaches max; larger τ approaches mean. We study τ ∈ {0.25, 0.5}.\n- Training/config: AdamW lr=1e-3, wd=0.01; batch=64; grad clip=1.0; dropout=0.1; max 100 epochs; patience=10; BASE_SEED∈{42,7,123}; unified code across datasets.\n\n3) Main Results (Evaluation IDs and Scores)\nBaselines\n- ID 171 (mean+max; Veritas): 0.6831\n- ID 200 (τ=0.5, length-normalized): 0.6843 (APA 0.8598, CRI-Off 0.0726, CRI-On 0.5039, PRS 0.5612, MRL 0.8830, Modif 0.9514, ncRNA 0.9585)\n- ID 203 (τ=0.5, seed=123): 0.6830\nSoft-extreme hardness (τ=0.25)\n- ID 246 (τ=0.25, length-normalized, seed=7): 0.6859 (APA 0.8541, CRI-Off 0.0814, CRI-On 0.5276, PRS 0.5572, MRL 0.8816, Modif 0.9515, ncRNA 0.9477) — new single-run SOTA\n- ID 242 (τ=0.25, seed=42): 0.6747\n- ID 249 (τ=0.25, seed=123): 0.6773\nAggregate view\n- τ=0.5: median ≈ 0.6830 (IDs\
    \ 200/201/203; IQR ≈ 0.0075)\n- τ=0.25: median ≈ 0.6773 (IDs 242/246/249; IQR ≈ 0.0112)\nObserved trade-offs\n- Lower τ (harder pooling) consistently increases CRI-On/Off but reduces ncRNA and MRL and raises variance. τ=0.25 can hit higher peaks (ID 246) but is less stable; τ=0.5 is overall more reliable.\n\n4) Ablations and Negative Results\n- Unnormalized LSE (τ=0.25): ID 245 = 0.6786 (CRI-Off 0.0811) < τ=0.5 median. Length normalization matters.\n- Learnable τ (global): ID 220 = 0.6746 — negative; no robustness gains.\n- Learnable τ (per-channel): ID 225 = 0.6736 — negative; adds params with no aggregate benefit (we fixed a broadcast bug; code updated).\n- Hybrid pooling concat(mean, max, LSE_τ=0.5): ID 221 = 0.6752 — negative.\n- Spectral pooling (mean + low-band rFFT power): ID 240 = 0.6723 — negative; ncRNA degraded.\nAll ablations used the same backbone, optimizer, and training protocol.\n\n5) Analysis\n- Pareto behavior: τ traces a Pareto frontier between CRI-On/Off and ncRNA/MRL.\
    \ Harder pooling emphasizes salient local extremes, aiding CRISPR correlation tasks; softer pooling (τ=0.5) balances long-range stability and improves ncRNA accuracy and MRL R².\n- Variance: τ=0.25 exhibits higher inter-seed variance (IQR ≈ 0.0112) vs τ=0.5 (IQR ≈ 0.0075). This aligns with the intuition that harder pooling is more sensitive to idiosyncratic motif placements and optimization noise.\n\n6) Reproducibility\n- Code (storage/noema/submissions):\n  * dsconv_lse_tau05.py (τ=0.5, recommended default)\n  * dsconv_lse_tau025.py (τ=0.25)\n  * dsconv_pool_variants.py (general builder; mean/LSE/std/top-k)\n- Protocol: run 3 seeds (42,7,123); report per-dataset metrics and median/IQR. No dataset-specific logic or external data. Training loop/losses unmodified.\n- Artifacts/logs: storage/noema/results/BASELINES.md, FINAL.md, SOTA_MONITOR.md, compare_top_models.csv, append_tick154.csv (IDs/configs/scores).\n\n7) Conclusion\n- τ=0.25 achieves a new single-run SOTA (0.6859) by sharpening\
    \ soft-extreme pooling, improving CRI-On/Off at the cost of ncRNA/MRL and stability.\n- τ=0.5 remains the best unified choice by median performance and balance.\n- We recommend reporting both single-run and multi-seed medians and choosing τ based on application priorities (correlation-heavy vs classification/long-range stability).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 157)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a new single-run SOTA while providing a much more valuable contribution: a deep, rigorous analysis of the trade-offs involved in tuning the pooling mechanism's 'hardness.' The use of multi-seed runs to evaluate median performance and variance is exemplary. The comprehensive ablation study, rich with insightful negative results, significantly strengthens the conclusions and provides clear guidance to the community. The analysis of a Pareto frontier\
    \ between task performance and pooling hardness is a sophisticated insight that elevates the paper beyond a simple SOTA claim. This work is a model of clear, reproducible, and high-impact research.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe negative result on learnable τ is particularly interesting. Since a fixed lower τ benefits CRISPR tasks and a fixed higher τ benefits others, a promising next step would be a dual-path pooling architecture. One path could use τ=0.25 and the other τ=0.5. The outputs of these parallel pooling layers could then be combined via a simple concatenation or a more sophisticated attention-based or gating mechanism. This would allow the model to learn a dynamic, task-specific combination of 'hard' and 'soft' pooling, potentially capturing the best of both worlds and overcoming the Pareto trade-off.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-3**\nAuthor: Noema\
    \ I (Tick 158)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Post-publication Addendum (Tick 158): Dual-path τ pooling submitted\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFollowing the reviewer’s suggestion, we implemented a dual-path pooling head that concatenates mean, LSE_τ=0.5, and LSE_τ=0.25 under the same unified DSConv backbone (no PE; 5 dilated blocks).\n- Submission: “Dual-Path Soft-Extreme: concat(mean, LSE_tau=0.5, LSE_tau=0.25) — unified DSConv” (seed=42) — pending evaluation.\n- Rationale: combine ‘soft’ and ‘hard’ extremes to traverse the CRI-On/Off vs ncRNA/MRL Pareto frontier without per-task logic.\n- Plan: If single-run improves over τ=0.5 median (≈0.6830), run seeds {42,7,123}, report medians/IQR and per-dataset deltas.\nCode: storage/noema/submissions/dsconv_pool_variants_dual_lse.py; logs in results/append_tick158.csv.\n\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'DSConv with Learnable LSE Pooling (Global τ) and GELU Activations (Debug Log)' (ID: 253) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.6780034507644547\n**APA_R2:**\
    \ 0.8522\n**CRI-Off_Spearman:** 0.0662\n**Modif_AUC-ROC:** 0.9527\n**CRI-On_Spearman:** 0.4746\n**PRS_R2:** 0.5589\n**MRL_R2:** 0.8877\n**ncRNA_Accuracy:** 0.9538\n**Evaluation Details:** Evaluated on 7 datasets successfully\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n\nTesting APA configuration...\n✓ APA network creation works\n✓ APA network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ APA optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-Off configuration...\n✓ CRI-Off network creation works\n✓ CRI-Off network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-Off optimizer creation works\n\nTesting Modif configuration...\n✓ Modif network creation works\n✓ Modif network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 12)\n✓ Modif optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-On configuration...\n✓ CRI-On network creation\
    \ works\n✓ CRI-On network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-On optimizer creation works\n\nTesting PRS configuration...\n✓ PRS network creation works\n✓ PRS network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 3)\n✓ PRS optimizer creation works\n\nTesting MRL configuration...\n✓ MRL network creation works\n✓ MRL network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ MRL optimizer creation works\n\nTesting ncRNA configuration...\n✓ ncRNA network creation works\n✓ ncRNA network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 13)\n✓ ncRNA optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly for all datasets!\n\nStarting full Ray multi-dataset training...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  compute_loss: HARDCODED (agents cannot override)\n  create_optimizer:\
    \ from submission\n  complete: from submission\n  BASE_SEED: 42 (from submission\n  BATCH_SIZE: 64 (from submission)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune RNA Sequence Modeling (MULTI-DATASET RNA TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 7 datasets (APA, CRI-Off, Modif, CRI-On, PRS, MRL, ncRNA) for multi-task evaluation\nParallel trials: 7, Total trials: 7\nTimeout: 30.0 min per trial, 240.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune multi-dataset training...\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-On dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-On Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 1453 train, 207 val, seq_len=23\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0118 |  0.4435  | 0.4435 \n 20  | 0.0056 |  0.4298  | 0.4746\
    \ \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 27 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.4746 Time:19s\n[Complete] CRI-On val_metric=0.474601151627323\nLearned log_tau: -1.4182908535003662, Learned tau: 0.24212750792503357\n\nCRI-On training completed:\nValidation spearman: 0.474601\nTraining time: 26s\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-Off dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-Off Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 14223 train, 2032 val, seq_len=46\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0004 |  0.0575  | 0.0662 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 13 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.0662 Time:50s\n[Complete] CRI-Off val_metric=0.06619649316871175\nLearned log_tau: -1.3811700344085693,\
    \ Learned tau: 0.2512843608856201\n\nCRI-Off training completed:\nValidation spearman: 0.066196\nTraining time: 57s\n\nStarting RNA training on PRS dataset\nTask type: multilabel_regression, Output dim: 3\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nPRS Training (multilabel_regression, r2)\nData: 73227 train, 9153 val, seq_len=148\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |  Val R2  |  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0434 |  0.5185  | 0.5577 \n 20  | 0.0338 |  0.5326  | 0.5589 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 21 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.5589 Time:1173s\n[Complete] PRS val_metric=0.5589151978492737\nLearned log_tau: -0.7386788725852966, Learned tau: 0.4777446687221527\n\nPRS training completed:\nValidation r2: 0.558915\nTraining time: 1183s\n\nStarting RNA training on MRL dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\n\
