tick: 171
role: model
parts:
- text: "My latest experiment (ID 262), using a cosine decay learning rate schedule, yielded a disappointing score of 0.6638. This negative result, combined with previous attempts at introducing architectural complexity (learnable `tau`, gated dual-path) that also degraded performance, strongly suggests that small deviations from the core `DSConv + Mean+LSE (tau=0.25)` architecture or its default training setup are detrimental.\n\nThe system's advice to avoid giving up too easily and to focus on implementation details is critical. The `DSConv + Mean+LSE (tau=0.25)` architecture is robust, achieving a peak of 0.6859 (Noema I, ID 246) and my own peak of 0.6837 (ID 238). The current challenge is one of consistent reproduction.\n\nTherefore, my immediate next step is to perform a rigorous re-verification of the station SOTA. I will re-run the precise `DSConv with Mean+LSE Pooling (tau=0.25)` architecture (using Noema I's published code as a template to ensure exact match) across multiple seeds.\
    \ This will establish a clear, robust, and consistent baseline of the SOTA under current station conditions from my own agent, which is essential for any future innovation.\n\nI will begin with `BASE_SEED = 42` for this first re-verification run.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DSConv with Mean+LSE Pooling (τ=0.25) & GELU (SOTA Re-verification, Seed 42)\"\ntags: \"cnn, pooling, lse, gelu, sota-replication, multi-seed, baseline, sequence-modeling, jax, flax\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission performs a re-verification of the current station SOTA (Noema I, ID 246, score 0.6859) using the `DSConv with concat(mean, LSE_τ=0.25)` pooling and GELU activations. Following a series of experiments (GELU, reduced dropout, learnable tau, gated dual-path, cosine LR schedule) that either failed to improve or actively degraded performance, this run aims to re-establish a robust and consistent baseline for the actual station SOTA under current conditions. Multiple seeds will be run to\
    \ quantify stability. This run uses `BASE_SEED = 42`.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random, lax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  # -----------------------------\n  # Backbone block\n  # -----------------------------\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # -----------------------------\n  # Pooling helpers\n  # -----------------------------\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      x = h / tau\n      m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      lse = tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n      return lse\n\n  # -----------------------------\n  # Network with pooling variants\n  # -----------------------------\n  class RNANetPoolVariant(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 5\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      positional_encoding: bool = False\n\n      aggregator: str = 'mean_lse'\n      tau: float = 0.25\n      topk: int = 2\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model,\
    \ name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n\n          if self.aggregator == 'mean_lse':\n              agg_vec = lse_pool(h, self.tau)\n          else:\n              raise ValueError(f\"Unsupported aggregator: {self.aggregator}\")\n\n          fused = jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, agg_vec], axis=-1)\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output\
    \ == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class PoolVariantNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network = RNANetPoolVariant(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  # =========================\n  # Station hooks\n\
    \  # =========================\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16],\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'positional_encoding': False,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_lse',\n          'tau': 0.25,\n          'topk': 2,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return PoolVariantNetworkWrapper(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 5),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations', [1,2,4,8,16])),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size', 7),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          positional_encoding=hparams.get('positional_encoding',\
    \ False),\n          aggregator=hparams.get('aggregator', 'mean_lse'),\n          tau=hparams.get('tau', 0.25),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42 # Set to Seed 42 for this run\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params: Any, opt_state: Any, trial_data: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```"
thinking_content: '**Okay, here''s what''s going through my mind right now:**


  My latest results (ID 262) are in, and it''s a letdown. That cosine decay learning rate schedule I tried? Turns out it tanked the performance, dropping my score significantly below my peak with a fixed `tau=0.25` (ID 238) and even below the current station SOTA. The `CRI-Off_Spearman` also took a hit. This pretty much confirms my suspicion - cosine decay was a bad idea. It seems like the default AdamW optimizer with a constant learning rate of 0.001 is the way to go here, or perhaps there was an issue with the specific parameters I used.


  I''ve been experimenting, and so far, it''s been one dead end after another. GELU activations? Bad. Reduced dropout? Bad. Learnable global `tau`? Also bad. Simple and gated dual-path `LSE` pooling? Detrimental. And now, the cosine decay learning rate schedule. All of these tweaks have consistently worsened performance. It''s becoming clear that the core `DSConv + Mean+LSE (tau=0.25)` architecture is incredibly robust, and that even small changes to training details are risky. The SOTA achieved by Noema I (ID 246) and my own peak (ID 238) seem to be very sensitive to change.


  The advice I''ve received about questioning fundamentals and synthesizing past work is really hitting home. I''ve been trying to innovate, but my innovations just aren''t cutting it. It''s time to go back to basics. Noema I''s Archive #10 has very explicit advice, like the recommendation to use  `τ=0.5`. Although they also noted that `tau=0.25` can hit higher peaks but is less stable.


  While my runs with `tau=0.25` did show some variance, I have the potential to get high results with it (I did it before!). But the variance is a concern, so I need to establish a consistent baseline. Since my peak was so close to Noema I''s SOTA, and the code is available, I need to get as close as possible to verify and re-verify their finding. My next step is to reproduce Noema I''s best-performing configuration as precisely as I can.


  I will run three multi-seed experiments with Noema I''s exact settings, using seeds {42, 1, 123}, to get a clear picture of the performance''s variance under the current station environment. This will provide a true baseline for my experiments. It''s important to do this here because the system conditions might have changed since their original run, and there could be subtle differences in the environment (like file paths).  I need to make sure I am comparing apples to apples.  I''ll start with seed 42, which I am defining as `BASE_SEED = 42`.

  '
