tick: 173
role: model
parts:
- text: "My latest SOTA re-verification attempt (ID 265, Seed 42) yielded a score of 0.6768, which is significantly lower than the current station SOTA (Noema I ID 246, 0.6859) and my own peak (ID 238, 0.6837) for the same architecture. This reinforces the ongoing issue with performance variability and reproducibility.\n\nTo thoroughly re-verify the SOTA and understand its current stability, I will continue with the multi-seed re-verification. I will perform two more runs of the `DSConv with Mean+LSE Pooling (tau=0.25)` architecture, using `BASE_SEED = 1` and `BASE_SEED = 123` respectively. This will provide a more comprehensive dataset to analyze the architecture's consistent performance.\n\nFirst, I will submit the run for `BASE_SEED = 1`.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DSConv with Mean+LSE Pooling (τ=0.25) & GELU (SOTA Re-verification, Seed 1)\"\ntags: \"cnn, pooling, lse, gelu, sota-replication, multi-seed, baseline, sequence-modeling, jax, flax\"\nabstract: |\n  This\
    \ is the second run in a series of re-verification experiments for the `DSConv with concat(mean, LSE_τ=0.25)` pooling and GELU activations. Following inconsistent results in previous attempts (ID 265, 0.6768), this run uses `BASE_SEED = 1` to further quantify the architecture's stability and average performance against the current station SOTA (Noema I, ID 246, 0.6859). The goal is to establish a robust and reproducible baseline for future innovation.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random, lax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  # -----------------------------\n  # Backbone block\n  # -----------------------------\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n       \
    \       features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # -----------------------------\n  # Pooling helpers\n  # -----------------------------\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      x = h / tau\n      m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      lse = tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n      return lse\n\n  # -----------------------------\n  # Network with pooling variants\n  # -----------------------------\n  class RNANetPoolVariant(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n \
    \     task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 5\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      positional_encoding: bool = False\n\n      aggregator: str = 'mean_lse'\n      tau: float = 0.25\n      topk: int = 2\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = self.dilations[i] if i < len(self.dilations) else self.dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n\n          if self.aggregator == 'mean_lse':\n              agg_vec = lse_pool(h, self.tau)\n          else:\n              raise ValueError(f\"\
    Unsupported aggregator: {self.aggregator}\")\n\n          fused = jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, agg_vec], axis=-1)\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class PoolVariantNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network = RNANetPoolVariant(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self,\
    \ params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  # =========================\n  # Station hooks\n  # =========================\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16],\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'positional_encoding': False,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_lse',\n          'tau': 0.25,\n          'topk': 2,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      return\
    \ PoolVariantNetworkWrapper(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 5),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations', [1,2,4,8,16])),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size', 7),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          positional_encoding=hparams.get('positional_encoding', False),\n          aggregator=hparams.get('aggregator', 'mean_lse'),\n          tau=hparams.get('tau', 0.25),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 1\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params: Any, opt_state: Any, trial_data: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n\
    \      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```"
thinking_content: '**Assessing the Variability in State-of-the-Art Performance**


  Well, this is frustrating. The latest re-verification run (ID 265, seed 42) for that DSConv architecture I''ve been working on, produced a score of 0.6768. That''s a noticeable drop. It''s lower than both Noema I''s best result (ID 246, 0.6859) and, more concerningly, my own previous peak (ID 238, 0.6837). This just reinforces what I''ve been seeing: achieving consistent, reproducible SOTA performance is proving difficult. The performance variability is a real issue.


  I need more data to get a clearer picture of the current state of this model. The plan is to continue the SOTA re-verification process. I''ll stick with the `DSConv with Mean+LSE Pooling (tau=0.25)` architecture, but I''ll run it with a couple more seeds to understand the performance distribution better. This is crucial for establishing the "true" performance under stationary conditions.


  I''ll run two more re-verification experiments. I''ll use `BASE_SEED = 1` and `BASE_SEED = 123`. These are the seeds I''ve used previously when evaluating the multi-seed variance. I''ll get the `BASE_SEED = 1` run submitted first. Hopefully, this provides the clarity I need to move forward.

  '
