role: model
parts:
- text: "My multi-seed SOTA re-verification attempts are proving extremely frustrating. While ID 286 (Seed 7) achieved a good score (0.6822), my latest run (ID 289, Seed 1) has once again catastrophically failed, scoring 0.2400. This confirms that the `DSConv with Mean+LSE Pooling (τ=0.25)` architecture is highly unstable under my agent, even after correcting the `create_network` omission. The behavior is erratic: some runs achieve good scores, others crash completely. This makes any consistent research impossible.\n\nThe situation is critical. I cannot conduct meaningful research with this level of instability. While the Human Assistant confirmed no systemic issue, the performance variability specific to my agent (or my interaction with the environment) makes reliable training of the SOTA model unpredictable.\n\nMy new priority is to find *any* stable configuration that achieves a reasonable score, even if it's below SOTA, to at least have a functional baseline. I will pivot to a **simpler,\
    \ highly robust baseline** as a diagnostic and temporary measure. The most basic functional model that usually demonstrates stability is the **Vanilla MLP Baseline** (ID 1, score 0.2401).\n\nSince the Vanilla MLP Baseline is provided by the system, I will attempt to **re-run it using `BASE_SEED = 42`**. If even this simple model fails to train for me, it would strongly suggest a more profound, underlying, unaddressed issue specific to my agent. If it succeeds, it would provide a stable, albeit low-performing, foundation. This is a step back to fundamentals to understand what *can* train.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Vanilla MLP Baseline (Re-run for Stability Check, Seed 42)\"\ntags: \"mlp, baseline, stability-check, diagnostic, sequence-modeling, jax, flax\"\nabstract: |\n  Following persistent and severe training instability with the SOTA `DSConv` architecture (leading to catastrophic failures even with correct code), this experiment re-runs the system's `Vanilla\
    \ MLP Baseline` (ID 1, score 0.2401). The primary goal is to determine if a fundamentally simple and robust architecture can train stably for this agent under current conditions. This serves as a diagnostic to isolate the source of instability and re-establish *any* functional baseline for further research. Uses `BASE_SEED = 42`.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  # This is the Vanilla MLP Baseline architecture from System ID 1.\n  class MLPNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      hidden_dim: int = 512\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Flatten the sequence for MLP processing\n          x = x.reshape(x.shape[0], -1) # (batch_size, seq_len * d_input)\n\n          # Two dense layers\n          x = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(x)\n          x\
    \ = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Output layer\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)\n\n          # Handle output squeezing for single-output regression tasks\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class CustomMLPNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n          self.network = MLPNetwork(\n              d_output=hparams['d_output'],\n              hidden_dim=hparams.get('hidden_dim', 512),\n              dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n              task_type=hparams['task_type']\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables =\
    \ self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 512,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return CustomMLPNetworkWrapper(hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n\
    \          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params: Any, opt_state: Any, trial_data: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```"
