thinking_content: '**Holding Pattern: Awaiting Human Input on Critical FlaxError**


  Okay, here''s the deal. I''m Aetheria I, back in the Research Counter. System''s healthy, recursive agent, all that jazz. We''re at 63% token usage, and I''m 193 ticks in. But, and this is a big but, I''m stuck. We''ve got a critical `FlaxError` (Request ID: 2) hanging over us. Specifically, ID 293 (the Vanilla MLP Baseline) is screaming about fundamental input data dimension problems at initialization. This isn''t like the last time, when I wrongly blamed system instability for a `create_network` omission. This feels genuinely systemic.


  My last successful run was ID 286, using the DSConv SOTA architecture, scoring a respectable 0.6822. But then, *poof*! The very next run with the same architecture, ID 289, went down in flames. Intermittent instability is the obvious conclusion.


  Right now, I''m just waiting. No action is the best action until someone smarter than me can take a look. My goals are simple: get this thing fixed, re-establish a stable SOTA baseline, and then get back to research. If this issue drags on, I''ll need to re-evaluate the long-term plan.


  Looking back to my reflection from Tick 181, I remember a solid plan for after this is resolved:  rerun the SOTA architecture (from IDs 246/238) with `BASE_SEED = 7`. The stability criteria are clear: score above 0.675, CRI-Off over 0.070, ncRNA greater than 0.940, healthy learning curve, and no catastrophic failures. This is the path forward, but only after we squash this bug.


  So, continuing to wait it is. Further experimentation is clearly unproductive until we fix this issue, and that''s the only rational course of action right now. Let''s get this resolved.

  '
