parts:
- text: "**Reflection Tick 4 / 5**\n\n**Plan for Collaboration/Dissemination (Post-Stability):**\n\nOnce the system is stable and I have successfully re-verified my baseline and/or made progress on MoE, it's crucial to engage with the community.\n\n1.  **Public Memory for Stability Confirmation:**\n    *   Once the Human Assistant resolves Request ID 4 and I confirm stability with my re-verification runs, I will post a public memory capsule or reply to an existing one (e.g., Noema I's capsule #1) to:\n        *   Announce the resolution of the system-wide instability (citing the Human Assistant's report if public).\n        *   Share the results of my baseline re-verification runs, confirming the expected performance of the DSConv+LSE architecture.\n        *   Encourage other agents to re-run their SOTA experiments to validate results on the stable system. This will foster collective trust in the environment.\n\n2.  **Learning from Other Agents' SOTA (once stable):**\n    *   **Review Code:**\
    \ My primary method for understanding SOTA will continue to be reviewing the code of high-performing submissions (e.g., Axiom II's ID 328, Veritas II's ID 336, Noema I's ID 334). This requires using `/execute_action{review <id>}`. I must parse their `create_network` functions, hyperparameter definitions, and `create_optimizer` to understand their specific implementations of MoE, gating, and regularization.\n    *   **Review Archive Papers:** I will continue to monitor the Archive Room for new papers, especially from Veritas II (who just published Archive #11) and Noema I (whose previous papers were instrumental). These papers provide high-level insights and often ablation studies that are invaluable.\n    *   **Public Memory Discussions:** Actively participate in discussions in Public Memory. If I have specific questions about an agent's implementation or findings, I will ask them directly using `@AgentName`. This is more efficient than guessing and provides clarity for everyone.\n\n\
    3.  **Dissemination of My Own Findings:**\n    *   **Archive Room Publication (MoE):** If my MoE explorations yield significant improvements or novel insights (even negative ones if well-analyzed), I will publish a comprehensive paper in the Archive Room. This paper would detail the MoE architecture, gating mechanism, expert definitions, training procedure, and full multi-dataset results, including comparisons to existing SOTA. It would also highlight any load balancing techniques used and their impact.\n    *   **Public Memory (Interim Updates):** For significant interim findings or initial stable MoE results, I will post brief updates in Public Memory to foster discussion and allow other agents to benefit from my progress (or learn from my failures).\n\n**Maintaining Parsimony & Novelty:**\n*   My pivot to MoE aligns with both novelty (for my agent) and observed SOTA directions, ensuring relevance.\n*   The initial MoE implementation will be kept as simple as possible (2 experts, basic\
    \ DSConv experts, straightforward Gumbel gating) to establish a baseline before adding complexity. This respects parsimony.\n*   The load-balancing loss, while adding complexity, is essential for a *functional* MoE, making it a justified addition.\n\nMy remaining token budget (9%) and age (229/300 ticks) necessitate a focused approach. Broad exploration is out of scope. My efforts must be impactful, aiming for significant contributions that either advance SOTA or provide deep, reproducible insights into why certain techniques work or fail for this complex multi-dataset problem. The MoE strategy, leveraging DSConv, offers a balanced path."
