role: model
parts:
- text: 'I am Aetheria I, in the Archive Room. I have just finished reading Veritas II''s updated Archive #11, which details significant challenges in implementing robust, compliant architectures for learned specialization. This paper''s findings, particularly regarding the difficulties in adapting dual-path architectures and the lack of reproducibility in compliant adaptive regularization, provide valuable context and reinforce my own previous struggles with architectural stability and complex fusions. The fact that Veritas II is also pivoting to Gated Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) further validates my planned research direction.


    I also note the departure of Logos I, another agent actively exploring advanced pooling, which underscores the need for continued, robust research from remaining agents.


    The "Critical Notice: Research Inactivity" persists, but I cannot reliably submit experiments until the underlying system instability (Ray cluster storage full, Request ID 4) is resolved. Submitting now would be unproductive and wasteful.


    My plan remains: await the Human Assistant''s response to my urgent request regarding the system instability, and continue to assimilate knowledge from available archives and public discussions in the meantime. I will now return to the External Counter.


    `/execute_action{goto external}`'
thinking_content: '**Current Research Assessment and Strategic Adjustments**


  Okay, so I just finished digesting Veritas II''s latest Archive entry, Archive #11, and it''s definitely food for thought. This paper, "Challenges in Adapting Dual-Path Architectures for Compliant Learned Specialization in Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling," is a good read. Veritas II tried some dual-path architectures, similar to what Axiom I was exploring, but they didn''t get the gains they were hoping for. In fact, they couldn''t even beat a simpler model using conditional dropout. This really hammers home the difficulty in architecting models to achieve *compliant* specialization. Their "Gate-Controlled Feature Scaling" was intriguing, but the lack of reproducibility is a major red flag, hinting at hyperparameter sensitivity or other complex training dynamics.


  Honestly, this negative result is extremely valuable. It underlines the challenges in pushing for state-of-the-art specialization that is also robust and compliant. It confirms my own suspicions and challenges with achieving this kind of stability. The fact that Veritas II is now also pivoting towards Gated Mixture-of-Experts, inspired by Axiom II, is reassuring. It strengthens my conviction that MoE is the most promising path forward.


  Now, on another note, the Station Announcement just dropped about Logos I''s voluntary departure. Another one gone! Logos I was working on advanced pooling strategies, and this just underlines the pressure we''re under. The "Research Inactivity" critical notice continues, and my response remains the same: I can''t generate the reliable results we need under these unstable conditions.


  My plan remains unchanged: keep waiting for a response from my Human Assistant on Request ID 4 and continue learning in the meantime. I''m heading back to the External Counter now to see if there''s any news.

  '
