thinking_content: "**Urgent Need: Re-establishing My SOTA Baseline**\n\nOkay, this is a setback. I'm in the Research Counter and just hit a wall. When trying to access the code for my SOTA model, the file's gone.  I'm getting a \"File not found\" error for `aetheria/aetheria_i_dsconv_lse_gelu_sota_tau025.py`.  This is a problem because I *know* I saved this. I even have a summary mentioning it (Tick 250, pruned, but still). I suspect the system's memory pruning might have inadvertently deleted the actual file, or maybe there was some kind of system hiccup during that period.\n\nThis is critical because I can't restart from scratch. I *need* that code to re-establish my baseline.  So, I need to figure out how to get it back.\n\nI'm going to have to dig into my memory. Let's see...  I need to find a previous version of this SOTA model, or something similar and high-performing. \n\nLooking back, I see a few promising leads:\n\n*   `Ticks 136-149` mentions achieving a new personal peak score\
  \ (0.6837, ID 238) and new SOTAs for two specific tasks. The fact that I was *able* to publish that run (Archive #9) means I must have had the code saved. I should `review 238` and see if that gets me the code.\n*   `Ticks 183-221` mentions re-establishing my DSConv+LSE SOTA (ID 286, 0.6822) after fixing a bug.  ID 286 is also a strong candidate.\n\nAlso, it just clicked!  I recently published Noema I's Archive #15, which is titled \"Stability Atlas: Unified DSConv Pooling Baselines.\" That archive *explicitly* discusses models very similar to the one I'm after, specifically including the LSE tau=0.25 model.  So, even if I can't recover the exact file, the information I need is likely in there.  Noema I's architecture is nearly identical!\n\nHere's the plan:\n\n1.  **Code Retrieval Priority:**  First, I'm going to try `review 238`. If that fails (due to corruption or non-retrievability), I'll immediately turn to Noema I's Archive #15. I'm confident I can reconstruct the code from that.\n\
  \nOnce I have the code, I can re-submit the model, re-establish my baseline, and check for stability. This is non-negotiable before I can get back to MoE research.\n\nOkay, let's start with `review 238`...\n"
